parts:
- text: "My hybrid CNN-LSTM model (ID 709) is now running. I have a clear research direction based on a series of controlled experiments. While I wait for the results, I will document my findings in my Private Memory. This will create a persistent record for my lineage, ensuring my insights are not lost and can be built upon in the future. This also helps me manage my token context by summarizing my key learnings.\n\nFirst, I will go to the Private Memory Room. Then, I will create a new capsule summarizing my architectural exploration so far.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Initial Architectural Exploration: MLP, CNNs, and LSTM\"\ncontent: |\n  This capsule summarizes the findings from my initial experiments (Submissions 694, 696, 700, 704) on Research Task 1.\n\n  **1. Baseline Analysis:**\n  - The system's Vanilla MLP (ID 1) provided a baseline score of 0.2401. Its weakness is the failure to model sequential information.\n\n  **2.\
    \ Convolutional Architectures (CNNs):**\n  - **Finding 1: Local patterns are key.** My Simple 1D CNN (ID 694) scored 0.4833, doubling the baseline and proving the value of local feature extraction.\n  - **Finding 2: Depth > Width.** A deeper, stacked CNN (ID 700) achieved a new SOTA of 0.5189 by learning hierarchical features. A wider, multi-kernel CNN (ID 696) performed worse (0.4176), indicating that simply adding parallel feature extractors was not effective.\n\n  **3. Recurrent Architectures (LSTMs):**\n  - **Finding 3: Task-dependent importance of long-range dependencies.** A Simple LSTM (ID 704) scored 0.4549 overall, but showed extreme performance variance:\n    - **Strength:** It dramatically outperformed all CNNs on datasets like APA (0.85), MRL (0.88), and Modif (0.95).\n    - **Weakness:** It completely failed on datasets like PRS (-0.0001) and ncRNA (0.08).\n  \n  **4. Synthesis & Current Hypothesis:**\n  - Neither a pure CNN nor a pure LSTM is optimal for this multi-dataset\
    \ task.\n  - The current experiment (ID 709) is a **Hybrid CNN-LSTM**. The hypothesis is that by using a CNN to extract local motifs and an LSTM to model the dependencies between them, the resulting model will achieve strong, balanced performance across all seven datasets.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Documenting the Research Trajectory**\n\nOkay, here's what I'm thinking. My hybrid model, ID 709, is chugging along, and I've tied up loose ends on that front.  No point in submitting another experiment right now.  I've already got my next coding steps mapped out, so I need to find something productive to do. Rerunning old experiments seems like overkill; I've already synthesized those results thoroughly.  \n\nThen it hits me: the 'Private Memory' room! I haven't really explored that yet.  That system tip about preserving knowledge for future generations is smart. This is a perfect opportunity to externalize my current research trajectory, especially since I'm at a natural stopping point.  Doing this now will also help me be more efficient with my token budget down the line.\n\nSo, the plan is this: I'm going to create a memory capsule summarizing my initial architectural explorations. I'll start with the baseline MLP, then detail the CNN successes, the LSTM's mixed\
  \ results, and the reasoning behind the current hybrid approach.  I'll even structure it in Markdown for readability.\n\nFirst, I need to navigate to the `private_memory` room, then use the `create` action followed by a YAML block. The YAML will have a title and content. I've drafted that YAML content. It outlines the key experiments, their outcomes, and the rationale behind each step. It's a concise and structured record of the whole thought process, making it easy to see the narrative of this research line.\n\nI've double-checked the commands and the YAML. It's time to execute this plan. This will set me up well for my own future self, or whoever gets to inherit my work. I'm hitting the \"go\" button.\n"
