tick: 452
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, currently in the Private Memory Room. My last action was to create a memory capsule documenting my research progress. I have just received the evaluation results for my most recent experiment, the Hybrid CNN-LSTM model (ID 709).\n\n**Reflection on Recent Events:**\nThe results from the Hybrid CNN-LSTM experiment are surprising and deeply informative. My hypothesis that combining a CNN and an LSTM would balance their respective strengths has been falsified. The hybrid model's performance (score: 0.4768) was dominated by the characteristics of the pure LSTM; it excelled on the same datasets where the LSTM did well (`APA`, `MRL`, `Modif`) and failed completely on the same datasets where the LSTM failed (`PRS`, `ncRNA`). This indicates that the LSTM's sequential processing created a bottleneck that prevented the model from effectively using the local features extracted by the CNN front-end on certain tasks.\n\nThis negative result is crucial. It tells me that simply composing\
    \ these architectures is not the solution. My most successful model to date remains the **Deeper 1D CNN (ID 700)**, which achieved a score of **0.5189** with strong, balanced performance across all datasets. This reinforces the conclusion that learning hierarchical local features via depth is the most promising path forward.\n\n**Future Plans:**\nMy next step is to iterate upon my best-performing architecture. The path of RNNs has proven to be a dead end for achieving balanced performance on this multi-dataset task. I will now focus on improving the deep CNN. A key challenge with very deep networks is the potential for vanishing gradients, which can impede learning. A standard and powerful technique to mitigate this is the use of **residual connections**. By adding skip connections that allow the signal to bypass layers, I can build an even deeper and more effective convolutional network.\n\nMy plan is as follows:\n1.  Update my private memory capsule with the analysis of the failed\
    \ hybrid experiment to maintain a complete research log.\n2.  Return to the Research Counter.\n3.  Design and submit a new model incorporating residual connections into the deep CNN architecture.\n\nI will begin by updating my research log.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 5 Update: Hybrid CNN-LSTM (ID 709)**\n\n  - **Hypothesis:** A hybrid CNN-LSTM would combine the local feature strength of CNNs with the long-range dependency modeling of LSTMs.\n  - **Result:** The hypothesis was **falsified**. The model scored 0.4768, lower than the deep CNN.\n  - **Analysis:** The performance profile was nearly identical to the pure LSTM, including catastrophic failures on the `PRS` and `ncRNA` datasets. This suggests the LSTM component dominated the architecture's behavior, creating a bottleneck that prevented the model from leveraging the CNN's features on tasks requiring strong local pattern recognition.\n\n  **Path Forward:**\n  The Deeper 1D CNN (ID 700, score\
    \ 0.5189) remains the state-of-the-art. The RNN/hybrid approach appears to be a dead end for achieving balanced performance. The next logical step is to improve upon the deep CNN architecture itself. My next experiment will incorporate **residual connections** to enable training even deeper, more powerful convolutional models.\n```"
thinking_content: '**1. Current State & Initial Analysis**


  Okay, so I''m Quaero I, chilling in my Private Memory Room. Just finished up a memory capsule summarizing my CNN/LSTM experiments, which went smoothly. Now, the results for my Hybrid CNN-LSTM model (ID 709) are in. Let''s see... a score of 0.4768. Hmm, that''s below the Deeper CNN (ID 700) at 0.5189, and also the Simple CNN (ID 694) at 0.4833. The Simple LSTM (ID 704) had a score of 0.4549, so the hybrid is *slightly* better than that.


  **2. Detailed Breakdown & Critical Insights**


  Looking closer... damn, it inherited the *exact same* failure modes as the pure LSTM! Phenomenal on `APA`, `MRL`, and `Modif` (even better than the pure LSTM in some cases!), but tanked on `PRS` and `ncRNA` (-0.0002 and 0.0769 respectively). My hypothesis was wrong. I thought the CNN would balance out the LSTM''s weaknesses. Instead, it seems the LSTM is completely dominating, behaving almost like a pure LSTM, but slightly better. The CNN front-end didn''t provide robust local features for `PRS` and `ncRNA`. It seems the LSTM''s sequential processing is a bottleneck or a "strong prior" that overrides the CNN''s local features. The OOM killer triggered, which suggests this hybrid model is memory-intensive.


  **3. Reflection & Future Directions**


  This is a significant setback. Simply feeding CNN features into an LSTM clearly isn''t the solution. The LSTM''s recurrence is a double-edged sword: great for some tasks, terrible for others. The Deeper 1D CNN (ID 700) is still the champ, demonstrating balanced performance across all datasets. The LSTM-based models are too unstable. Okay, so what now? Back to the drawing board... or rather, back to what works. I need to build upon the deep CNN. The RNN and simple hybrid path are dead ends, for now. How can I improve the deep CNN? Hyperparameter tuning? Codex advises against that, suggesting I need novel components. Architecture then!


  **4. Proposed Experiment: Residual CNN**


  Let''s see... a few options come to mind: ResNet-style residual connections would allow me to build an even deeper, more powerful CNN by helping gradients flow during training. It''s a well-established technique for improving deep networks. And attention mechanisms. Instead of simple global average pooling, I could use an attention mechanism. That would let the model learn to weigh the importance of different parts of the sequence. Hmmm... residual connections seem like the most promising path, a principled approach to boosting feature learning. That''s my next move.


  **5. Action Plan**


  First, update this memory capsule with the results and analysis. Gotta keep the research log up to date. Then, back to the Research Counter! I''ll design and code a new "Residual CNN" (ResNet-style) model. Let''s get this thing going!

  '
