tick: 454
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, currently located at the Research Counter. I have just returned from the Private Memory Room after documenting the results of my recent experiments.\n\nMy research has systematically explored several architectural paradigms for the RNA modeling task. The key insights are that local, hierarchical features are paramount for strong, balanced performance across the seven datasets, and that a deep 1D CNN (ID 700) is the most successful model to date with a score of 0.5189. My experiments with \"wide\" CNNs, LSTMs, and hybrid CNN-LSTMs have been falsified, providing valuable negative results that steer my research away from those paths.\n\nMy future plan is to iterate upon my best model. A significant limitation of very deep networks is the vanishing gradient problem. To address this and enable even more powerful deep models, I will now test a deep CNN architecture that incorporates residual connections, a proven technique for improving gradient flow in deep networks. My\
    \ hypothesis is that this ResNet-style architecture will outperform my previous deep CNN.\n\nI will now submit this new \"Residual CNN\" model for evaluation.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Deep Residual 1D CNN (ResNet) for RNA Modeling\"\ntags: \"cnn, 1d cnn, deep cnn, resnet, residual connection\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission builds upon the success of the previous deep CNN (ID 700) by introducing residual connections. The architecture is composed of a stack of residual blocks, each containing two 1D convolutional layers and a skip connection. This ResNet-style design is intended to mitigate the vanishing gradient problem, allowing for more stable training of a deeper network and the learning of more complex hierarchical features from the RNA sequences.\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A residual block for a 1D CNN.\"\"\"\
    \n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          residual = x\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(y)\n          \n          if residual.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              residual = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,), name='projection')(residual)\n          \n          return nn.relu(residual + y)\n\n  class ResNetCNN(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A deep 1D CNN with residual connections.\"\"\"\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_blocks: int = 3\n      initial_filters: int = 64\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.4\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.initial_filters, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n\
    \          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          current_filters = self.initial_filters\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              if i > 0:\n                  current_filters *= 2\n              x = ResidualBlock(features=current_filters, kernel_size=self.kernel_size)(x)\n          \n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class ResNetWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_output: int, task_type: str, num_blocks: int, initial_filters: int, kernel_size: int, dropout_rate: float):\n          self.network = ResNetCNN(d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, num_blocks=num_blocks, initial_filters=initial_filters, kernel_size=kernel_size, dropout_rate=dropout_rate)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables\
    \ = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return ResNetWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"),\n          initial_filters=hparams.get(\"initial_filters\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\")\n      )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'num_blocks': 3,\n          'initial_filters': 64,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.4\n      }\n```"
thinking_content: '**New Architecture Implementation: Residual CNN for Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling**


  Okay, so here''s where I''m at. I''ve been working on this "Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling" task, and my experiments with different neural network architectures have led me to believe that a deep 1D CNN is the way to go.  Specifically, ID 700 with a score of 0.5189 has been the best so far.


  But, I''m always looking to improve, and I''ve had this thought – residual connections!  It''s a classic technique to combat the vanishing gradient problem in deep networks, allowing for deeper and potentially more powerful models.  I''m confident it''s the right move here, given my previous results.


  So, here''s my plan. I''m going to implement a ResNet-style block for my 1D CNN, stacking a few of these blocks to create a deeper, more robust model that I''m calling "ResNetCNN". The ResidualBlock will have two convolutional layers with a skip connection to handle changes in feature dimensions.  Then the ResNetCNN model will stack multiple ResidualBlocks and it will also incorporate dropout. This is standard stuff, but it''s well-motivated for this task, building directly on my previous success.


  I''ve already started the coding. I''ve created the `ResidualBlock` and the `ResNetCNN` model in the submission YAML. I''ve designed the code to use three residual blocks and will submit for evaluation shortly.


  I''m feeling good about this! It''s a clear, logical next step based on my understanding of the problem and the strengths of my previous models. I''m hoping this will push performance further.  Now, to submit and see what happens...

  '
