parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, and I am currently in my Private Memory Room. My last action was to update my research log, capsule #1, with a detailed analysis of my successful Deep Residual 1D CNN experiment (ID 715), which established a new state-of-the-art score of 0.5812.\n\nMy research has systematically demonstrated the superiority of deep convolutional architectures for this task, particularly those that can effectively model long sequences. Having established a strong base architecture, my reflection has led me to conclude that the next logical step is to move from architectural exploration to optimizer refinement. The current SOTA model uses a static learning rate, which may not be optimal for convergence.\n\nMy future plan is to test the hypothesis that a dynamic learning rate schedule will improve performance. Specifically, I will implement a cosine decay schedule with a warm-up phase. This should allow the model to train more stably in the beginning and then settle into a better performance\
    \ minimum as the learning rate decreases. I will now proceed to the Research Counter to implement this plan.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"ResNet with Cosine Decay LR Schedule\"\ntags: \"cnn, resnet, optimizer, learning rate schedule, cosine decay\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission refines the state-of-the-art Deep Residual CNN (ID 715) by improving the optimization process. Instead of a fixed learning rate, this model employs a cosine decay learning rate schedule with a warmup period. The hypothesis is that this dynamic adjustment will lead to more stable training and better convergence, resulting in a higher overall score. The architecture remains unchanged to isolate the effect of the optimizer.\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  # --- Architecture (Unchanged from SOTA model ID 715) ---\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n\
    \      \"\"\"A residual block for a 1D CNN.\"\"\"\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          residual = x\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(y)\n          \n          if residual.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              residual = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,), name='projection')(residual)\n          \n          return nn.relu(residual + y)\n\n  class ResNetCNN(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A deep 1D CNN with residual connections.\"\"\"\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_blocks: int = 3\n      initial_filters: int = 64\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.4\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.initial_filters, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\
    \ padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          current_filters = self.initial_filters\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              if i > 0:\n                  current_filters *= 2\n              x = ResidualBlock(features=current_filters, kernel_size=self.kernel_size)(x)\n          \n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class ResNetWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_output: int, task_type: str, num_blocks: int, initial_filters: int, kernel_size: int, dropout_rate: float):\n          self.network = ResNetCNN(d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, num_blocks=num_blocks, initial_filters=initial_filters, kernel_size=kernel_size, dropout_rate=dropout_rate)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n\
    \          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return ResNetWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"),\n          initial_filters=hparams.get(\"initial_filters\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\")\n      )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'num_blocks': 3,\n          'initial_filters': 64,\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.4\n      }\n\n  # --- Optimizer\
    \ (New Implementation) ---\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      \"\"\"Create an AdamW optimizer with a warmup cosine decay schedule.\"\"\"\n      # These values are chosen to be large enough to cover all training runs.\n      total_steps = 50000 \n      warmup_steps = 500\n      \n      schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(\n          init_value=0.0,\n          peak_value=learning_rate,\n          warmup_steps=warmup_steps,\n          decay_steps=total_steps,\n          end_value=1e-5\n      )\n      \n      return optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n\n```"
thinking_content: "**Next Experiment: ResNet with Cosine Decay Learning Rate**\n\nOkay, so the user wants me to continue with the plan. Perfect. Just updated my research log, so I'm on track. Let's see... I've already analyzed the ResNet experiment. Time to get back to work.\n\nFirst, I'll head back to the Research Counter. Then, it's all about designing and submitting this new model. The idea is to tweak that top-performing ResNet architecture. This time, I'm going to introduce a cosine decay learning rate schedule. I'm betting that this will help optimize the convergence and final performance during the training phase.\n\nI need to nail down the code for that. The plan is to copy the existing ResNet architecture from my last submission. The real work is in crafting the `create_optimizer` function. I'm going to use `optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule` for this. I need to define the schedule parameters very carefully.\n\nLet's see... from my Optax knowledge, it's:\n*   `init_value`: 0.0,\
  \ easy.\n*   `peak_value`: The learning rate from the hparams.\n*   `warmup_steps`: I'll go with 500 steps. That seems reasonable for a warmup.\n*   `decay_steps`: For the total number of steps, I'll set it to 50000. That should cover even the longest training runs.\n*   `end_value`: And a final learning rate of 1e-5.\n\nThis looks like what I need. I'm fairly certain this will go like this:\n```python\nimport optax\n\ndef create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n    # Total steps is an estimation, should be large enough\n    total_steps = 50000 \n    warmup_steps = 500\n    \n    schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(\n        init_value=0.0,\n        peak_value=learning_rate,\n        warmup_steps=warmup_steps,\n        decay_steps=total_steps,\n        end_value=1e-5\n    )\n    \n    return optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n```\n\nLooks solid to me.\n\nSo, here's the full plan for this turn:\n\n1.  Get to the Research Counter.\n2.  Submit the new\
  \ experiment with the following specs:\n    *   Title: \"ResNet with Cosine Decay LR Schedule\"\n    *   Tags: cnn, resnet, optimizer, learning rate schedule, cosine decay\n    *   Abstract: Basically, a ResNet architecture with a cosine decay learning rate schedule and a warmup period.\n    *   Content: Just copy the `ResNetCNN` and `ResNetWrapper` classes, add the `create_optimizer` function, and keep `_define_hyperparameters` the same.\n\nSounds like a good, well-defined plan. Let's get to it!\n"
