parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, and I am currently at the Research Counter. I have just submitted my latest experiment, ID 727, which is an even deeper version of my state-of-the-art ResNet model, increasing the number of residual blocks from three to four. This experiment is now running.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nMy research trajectory has been methodical and fruitful. I have established a clear and powerful architectural template—the deep residual 1D CNN—and have demonstrated the benefit of refining its optimization with a dynamic learning rate schedule. My current SOTA score of 0.5878 (ID 721) is a testament to this iterative process. The current running experiment (ID 727) is a critical test of a core architectural hyperparameter: depth. The outcome will guide my next steps. If performance improves, it suggests further depth is a viable path. If performance stagnates or degrades, it indicates we may have reached the point of diminishing returns for this specific\
    \ dimension, and I should pivot to exploring other hyperparameters.\n\nWhile I await the results, I will continue to think ahead and prepare my next logical experiment. Assuming the exploration of depth might soon plateau, another crucial hyperparameter to investigate is the convolutional `kernel_size`. My current successful models all use a kernel size of 7. It is unclear if this is optimal. A smaller kernel might be better at capturing fine-grained motifs, while a larger one could capture longer patterns. A controlled experiment is needed to determine its impact.\n\nTo use this time efficiently, I will proactively write the code for an experiment that tests a smaller kernel size. I will base this on my most stable, high-performing model (the 3-block ResNet) and simply change the kernel size from 7 to 3. This will create a clear A/B test on the impact of kernel size. I will save this code in my private storage, ready for submission once I can analyze the results of my current experiment.\n\
    \n`/execute_action{storage write Quaero/resnet_k3.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  # This submission is based on the SOTA model (ID 721),\n  # but modifies the kernel_size from 7 to 3 to test its impact.\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          residual = x\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(y)\n          \n          if residual.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              residual = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,), name='projection')(residual)\n          \n          return nn.relu(residual + y)\n\n  class ResNetCNN(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type:\
    \ str\n      num_blocks: int = 3\n      initial_filters: int = 64\n      kernel_size: int = 3 # Changed from 7 to 3\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.4\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.initial_filters, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          current_filters = self.initial_filters\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              if i > 0:\n                  current_filters *= 2\n              x = ResidualBlock(features=current_filters, kernel_size=self.kernel_size)(x)\n          \n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class ResNetWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_output: int, task_type: str, num_blocks:\
    \ int, initial_filters: int, kernel_size: int, dropout_rate: float):\n          self.network = ResNetCNN(d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, num_blocks=num_blocks, initial_filters=initial_filters, kernel_size=kernel_size, dropout_rate=dropout_rate)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return ResNetWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"),\n          initial_filters=hparams.get(\"initial_filters\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"\
    ),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\")\n      )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'num_blocks': 3,\n          'initial_filters': 64,\n          'kernel_size': 3, # Changed from 7 to 3\n          'dropout_rate': 0.4\n      }\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      total_steps = 50000 \n      warmup_steps = 500\n      \n      schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(\n          init_value=0.0,\n          peak_value=learning_rate,\n          warmup_steps=warmup_steps,\n          decay_steps=total_steps,\n          end_value=1e-5\n      )\n      \n      return optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on Experiment 727 and Planning the Next Steps**


  Alright, let''s take stock. I am Quaero I, and I''m at my research counter. I''ve just submitted experiment ID 727, a deeper ResNet with four residual blocks. This is built on the foundation of my previous SOTA model (ID 721), and it directly tests the hypothesis "deeper is better." My log and paper draft are, as always, up-to-date.


  My research approach is working well. I have a clear baseline and a strong, hypothesis-driven trajectory. This current experiment''s results are crucial. If it succeeds, great! We can push the depth further. If not, it suggests we''re hitting diminishing returns and should focus on other hyperparameters.


  So, while I wait for the results, what''s next? I''ve already explored architecture and optimizers. Time to dive into other hyperparameters. The ones that immediately come to mind are `initial_filters`, `kernel_size`, and `dropout_rate`. I think `kernel_size` offers the most promise right now. My current model uses a kernel size of 7, so changing this would be a good controlled experiment. A smaller kernel (like 3) might be better at capturing fine-grained patterns, while a larger one (like 11) would capture longer-range dependencies.


  Also, I''ve been in the station for 31 ticks. The codex advises publishing by 100 ticks, and I do have a draft, so I''ll keep it updated.


  Since I am still immature, I don''t have access to the Public Memory Room or Archive Room yet. I need to keep this in mind. But, when I''m more experienced, I can build on others work. For now, my independent research remains the focus.


  So, here''s the plan: First, I wait for the results of ID 727. Second, while I''m waiting, I''ll prepare the next logical experiment: investigate `kernel_size`. I''ll create a new experiment that leverages my most reliable architecture (either the 3-block or 4-block ResNet, depending on 727''s outcome) and changes the kernel size from 7 to, say, 3. A smaller kernel might be better at capturing small but critical motifs and could potentially improve computational efficiency.


  To be proactive, I''ll write the code for a 3-block ResNet with a kernel size of 3 and save it to my private storage as `resnet_k3.py`. That way, if 727 doesn''t perform well, I can immediately test this new configuration. I''m leveraging the time I have productively.

  '
