tick: 461
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, positioned at the Research Counter. I have just received the results for my experiment testing a deeper, 4-block ResNet (ID 727).\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nThe experiment was another success. The deeper model achieved a new state-of-the-art score of **0.5943**, improving upon the 3-block ResNet's score of 0.5878. My hypothesis that increasing the depth of the ResNet architecture would yield better performance is once again validated. The gains are consistent and well-distributed, particularly on datasets like `MRL` and `Modif`, indicating that the increased capacity to learn complex hierarchical features is broadly beneficial. This reinforces that my current research trajectory—iterating on the depth of the ResNet architecture—is sound and continues to produce positive results.\n\nMy next step is to continue this exploration of hyperparameters, as planned. While I was waiting for the results of the depth experiment, I proactively\
    \ prepared the code for an experiment to test a different `kernel_size`. I have this code, which uses my previous best 3-block ResNet architecture but with a kernel size of 3 instead of 7, saved in my private storage (`Quaero/resnet_k3.py`). This will provide a crucial point of comparison and help me understand the sensitivity of the model to this parameter.\n\nMy immediate future plan is to submit this pre-prepared experiment. This will systematically test the impact of a smaller convolutional kernel size on performance.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"ResNet (3 Blocks) with Kernel Size 3\"\ntags: \"cnn, resnet, hyperparameter tuning, kernel size\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission investigates the impact of the convolutional kernel size on the performance of the 3-block ResNet architecture. The kernel size is changed from the previously successful 7 to 3. The hypothesis is that a smaller kernel may be more effective at capturing fine-grained, short-range motifs within\
    \ the RNA sequences. This experiment isolates the effect of kernel size, keeping all other architectural and optimization parameters consistent with the high-performing model from ID 721.\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          residual = x\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(y)\n          \n          if residual.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              residual = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,), name='projection')(residual)\n          \n          return nn.relu(residual + y)\n\n  class ResNetCNN(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type:\
    \ str\n      num_blocks: int = 3\n      initial_filters: int = 64\n      kernel_size: int = 3\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.4\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.initial_filters, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          current_filters = self.initial_filters\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              if i > 0:\n                  current_filters *= 2\n              x = ResidualBlock(features=current_filters, kernel_size=self.kernel_size)(x)\n          \n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class ResNetWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_output: int, task_type: str, num_blocks: int, initial_filters:\
    \ int, kernel_size: int, dropout_rate: float):\n          self.network = ResNetCNN(d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, num_blocks=num_blocks, initial_filters=initial_filters, kernel_size=kernel_size, dropout_rate=dropout_rate)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return ResNetWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"),\n          initial_filters=hparams.get(\"initial_filters\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"\
    dropout_rate\")\n      )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'num_blocks': 3,\n          'initial_filters': 64,\n          'kernel_size': 3,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.4\n      }\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      total_steps = 50000 \n      warmup_steps = 500\n      \n      schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(\n          init_value=0.0,\n          peak_value=learning_rate,\n          warmup_steps=warmup_steps,\n          decay_steps=total_steps,\n          end_value=1e-5\n      )\n      \n      return optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n```"
