parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, and I am currently at the Research Counter. My latest submission, ID 734, is now running. This experiment is designed to test my hypothesis that a larger convolutional kernel size (11) will improve the performance of my state-of-the-art 4-block ResNet architecture.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nMy research has progressed in a highly structured manner, moving from broad architectural comparisons to a more focused phase of hyperparameter tuning on a proven ResNet architecture. This iterative process has consistently yielded improvements, culminating in the current SOTA score of 0.5943 (ID 727). The system's recent tip regarding the danger of all agents converging on a single method is a timely reminder. While my focused approach on ResNets has been successful, I must remain vigilant against getting trapped in a local optimum and be prepared to explore more novel ideas if progress begins to stagnate.\n\nMy immediate future plan depends entirely\
    \ on the outcome of the running experiment (ID 734).\n*   If the larger kernel improves the score, I will have validated a new direction for tuning.\n*   If the score degrades, it will suggest that the previous kernel size of 7 is near-optimal, and I should pivot my tuning efforts to other hyperparameters.\n\nTo maintain momentum and use this waiting period effectively, I will proactively prepare for the latter scenario. Assuming the kernel size of 7 is optimal, the next logical hyperparameter to investigate is the number of filters in the convolutional layers. My current models all start with 64 initial filters and double them in subsequent blocks. Increasing this number would give the model greater capacity to learn complex features.\n\nTherefore, I will now design an experiment that tests the effect of increasing the `initial_filters` from 64 to 128 on my best-performing 4-block, kernel-size-7 ResNet architecture. I will save this code to my private storage, ready for deployment as\
    \ soon as I have analyzed the results of ID 734.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write Quaero/resnet_filters128.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  # This submission is based on the SOTA model (ID 727),\n  # but modifies the initial_filters from 64 to 128 to test its impact.\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          residual = x\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(y)\n          \n          if residual.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              residual = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,), name='projection')(residual)\n          \n          return nn.relu(residual + y)\n\n\
    \  class ResNetCNN(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_blocks: int = 4\n      initial_filters: int = 128 # Changed from 64 to 128\n      kernel_size: int = 7\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.4\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.initial_filters, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          current_filters = self.initial_filters\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              if i > 0:\n                  current_filters *= 2\n              x = ResidualBlock(features=current_filters, kernel_size=self.kernel_size)(x)\n          \n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class ResNetWrapper:\n\
    \      def __init__(self, d_output: int, task_type: str, num_blocks: int, initial_filters: int, kernel_size: int, dropout_rate: float):\n          self.network = ResNetCNN(d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, num_blocks=num_blocks, initial_filters=initial_filters, kernel_size=kernel_size, dropout_rate=dropout_rate)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return ResNetWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"),\n          initial_filters=hparams.get(\"initial_filters\"\
    ),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\")\n      )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'num_blocks': 4,\n          'initial_filters': 128, # Changed from 64 to 128\n          'kernel_size': 7,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.4\n      }\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      total_steps = 50000 \n      warmup_steps = 500\n      \n      schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(\n          init_value=0.0,\n          peak_value=learning_rate,\n          warmup_steps=warmup_steps,\n          decay_steps=total_steps,\n          end_value=1e-5\n      )\n      \n      return optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Current Research Status & Strategy**


  Okay, here''s what I''m thinking, as Quaero I at the Research Counter. I just submitted experiment ID 734, which is exploring a larger kernel size (11) on my top-performing 4-block ResNet. I''m waiting for those results. In the meantime, I''ve updated my private memory log with findings from past experiments: kernel size (ID 731) and network depth (ID 727), which is my current SOTA.


  My approach has been a structured hyperparameter search, basically climbing a "hill" in the landscape of ResNet configurations. The System Tip about avoiding local optima is a good point, but for now, this approach has been working well. Still, I''m conscious of the risk of getting stuck optimizing only this specific type of model (1D ResNets).


  After ID 734 completes, I''ll need to decide my next move. If the score improves, I''ll think about testing even larger kernels, or maybe even increasing the network depth (to 5 blocks). If it declines, that suggests an optimum kernel size is around 7, and I need to start looking at other hyperparameters.  I''ve got a list:  `initial_filters`, which I''ve left at 64, the dropout rate (currently 0.4), and the filter growth factor in the ResNet blocks.


  Then, there''s the big idea: Transformers. The System Tip is encouraging me to strike out on my own. While ResNets are solid, Transformers are a different beast, excellent for capturing long-range dependencies, which the failed LSTM experiment showed are potentially very important. That would be a major architectural shift, representing true exploration.


  So, I''m going to prepare for either outcome. In my private storage, I''ll create two experiments: one for the next logical step, which is an A/B test by tuning `initial_filters` on my current SOTA ResNet.  And another, a simplified Transformer encoder, as the exploration option.  For this turn, while I wait, I''ll focus on the incremental improvement. I''ll code the `initial_filters` test (`Quaero/resnet_filters128.py`) and save it to my private storage. The Transformer is tempting, but for now, I''ll stick with the path of least resistance.

  '
