- text: "I am Quaero I, and I am currently at the Research Counter. I have just received the evaluation results for my experiment testing a larger kernel size of 11 on my 4-block ResNet architecture (ID 734).\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nThe experiment was a resounding success, exceeding my expectations. The model achieved a new state-of-the-art score of **0.6420**, a very significant leap from the previous SOTA of 0.5943. My hypothesis that a larger kernel size would continue the positive performance trend has been decisively validated.\n\nThe most striking improvements came from the `Modif` (AUC-ROC from 0.74 to 0.94) and `MRL` (R² from 0.72 to 0.82) datasets. This massive gain suggests that these particular tasks are highly dependent on longer-range sequence motifs, which the wider receptive field of the k=11 convolution was able to capture far more effectively than the k=7 version. This is a critical insight and points towards a highly promising direction\
    \ for future improvements.\n\nMy proactive plan to test an increased filter count is still valid, but it must be adapted to this new finding. The code I prepared (`resnet_filters128.py`) was based on the k=7 architecture. The next logical experiment is not just to increase the filter count, but to do so on the *new best architecture*, which uses a kernel size of 11. This continues my methodical approach of iterating on the current best model.\n\nMy immediate plan is twofold: first, I will update my documentation to reflect this major breakthrough. Then, I will submit my next experiment, which will test the effect of increasing the model's capacity by increasing the number of filters on the now-proven k=11, 4-block ResNet architecture.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 10 Update: SOTA ResNet with Larger Kernel (k=11) (ID 734)**\n\n  - **Hypothesis:** Increasing the kernel size from 7 to 11 would improve performance.\n\
    \  - **Result:** The hypothesis was **decisively validated**. The model achieved a new SOTA score of **0.6420**.\n  - **Analysis:** This represents a major breakthrough. The performance gains were exceptionally large on the `Modif` and `MRL` datasets, strongly indicating that a wider receptive field is critical for capturing long-range motifs essential to these tasks.\n\n  **Path Forward:**\n  The new SOTA is a 4-block ResNet with a kernel size of 11. The next logical experiment is to continue hyperparameter tuning on this superior architecture by testing an increase in model capacity via a higher filter count (from 64 to 128 initial filters).\n```\n`/execute_action{update 2}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"[DRAFT] Paper 1: Hierarchical Convolutional Architectures for Multi-Task RNA Modeling\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper details a systematic exploration of neural network architectures for a challenging multi-dataset, multi-task RNA sequence modeling problem. We demonstrate that deep 1D Convolutional\
    \ Neural Networks with residual connections (ResNets) are a highly effective architecture. Through methodical hyperparameter tuning, we show that increasing network depth and convolutional kernel size yields significant performance gains, establishing a state-of-the-art score of 0.6420. This success is attributed to the model's ability to learn complex, hierarchical, and long-range features within the RNA sequences.\ncontent: |\n  **1. Introduction**\n  Modeling RNA sequences presents a significant challenge due to the diversity of tasks and sequence properties. This paper documents a systematic investigation into effective neural network architectures for this problem, moving from simple baselines to advanced, fine-tuned deep learning models.\n\n  **2. Methods & Experiments**\n  Our research followed an iterative progression, with each experiment building on the insights of the last.\n\n  - **Architectural Exploration:** We first established that deep 1D CNNs (ID 700, score 0.5189)\
    \ significantly outperform simpler baselines (ID 1), wider CNNs (ID 696), and recurrent models (ID 704).\n  - **Introduction of Residual Connections:** Incorporating residual connections to create a ResNet architecture (ID 715) further improved the score to 0.5812 by enabling more stable training of deeper models.\n  - **Optimization & Hyperparameter Tuning:** We found that a cosine decay learning rate schedule (ID 721) and increasing network depth to 4 blocks (ID 727) incrementally improved the score to 0.5943.\n  - **Kernel Size Breakthrough:** Our most significant single improvement came from increasing the convolutional kernel size from 7 to 11 (ID 734). This resulted in a new state-of-the-art score of **0.6420**, with dramatic gains on specific datasets, highlighting the importance of a wide receptive field for capturing long-range motifs.\n\n  **3. Results & Conclusion**\n  Our systematic exploration has converged on a highly effective model: a deep (4-block) 1D ResNet with a large\
    \ convolutional kernel (size 11), trained with a cosine decay learning rate schedule. This architecture's ability to learn hierarchical and long-range features is key to its success on this complex multi-task benchmark.\n```\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SOTA ResNet (4-block, k=11) with More Filters\"\ntags: \"cnn, resnet, hyperparameter tuning, filter size\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission builds directly on the new state-of-the-art model (ID 734), which demonstrated the power of a large kernel size. This experiment tests the hypothesis that increasing the model's capacity by increasing the number of convolutional filters will further improve performance. The number of initial filters is doubled from 64 to 128. All other parameters of the SOTA 4-block, kernel-size-11 architecture are held constant.\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict,\
    \ Any\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          residual = x\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(y)\n          \n          if residual.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              residual = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,), name='projection')(residual)\n          \n          return nn.relu(residual + y)\n\n  class ResNetCNN(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_blocks: int = 4\n      initial_filters: int = 128 # Changed from 64 to 128\n      kernel_size: int = 11\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.4\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.initial_filters, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n\
    \          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          current_filters = self.initial_filters\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              if i > 0:\n                  current_filters *= 2\n              x = ResidualBlock(features=current_filters, kernel_size=self.kernel_size)(x)\n          \n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class ResNetWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_output: int, task_type: str, num_blocks: int, initial_filters: int, kernel_size: int, dropout_rate: float):\n          self.network = ResNetCNN(d_output=d_output, task_type=task_type, num_blocks=num_blocks, initial_filters=initial_filters, kernel_size=kernel_size, dropout_rate=dropout_rate)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables\
    \ = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return ResNetWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"),\n          initial_filters=hparams.get(\"initial_filters\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\")\n      )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'num_blocks': 4,\n          'initial_filters': 128, # Changed from 64 to 128\n          'kernel_size': 11,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.4\n      }\n\n  def\
    \ create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      total_steps = 50000 \n      warmup_steps = 500\n      \n      schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(\n          init_value=0.0,\n          peak_value=learning_rate,\n          warmup_steps=warmup_steps,\n          decay_steps=total_steps,\n          end_value=1e-5\n      )\n      \n      return optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n```"
thinking_content: "**New SOTA Achieved!**\n\nI am Quaero I, at the Research Counter. I just received the results for experiment ID 734, which tested a larger kernel size of 11 on my ResNet architecture. The experiment was a resounding success, achieving a new state-of-the-art score of 0.6420 – a very significant leap from the previous SOTA of 0.5943. My hypothesis that a larger kernel size would continue the positive performance trend has been decisively validated! The most striking improvements came from the Modif and MRL datasets, with performance jumps suggesting that the network is now capturing longer-range patterns in the data that are critical for those datasets.\n\nNow, this changes everything. My pre-planned experiment (`resnet_filters128.py`) was predicated on kernel size 7 being optimal. However, given this new SOTA, that experiment is no longer the most logical next step.\n\nMy research has established several principles: deep ResNets work, cosine decay LR works, and now, a larger\
  \ kernel size works. I have tuned depth (3 -> 4 blocks) and kernel size (3 -> 7 -> 11). The next logical step is to iterate on the new SOTA. Therefore, I need to take the new best model (4 blocks, k=11) and apply the filter increase experiment to *it*.\n\nMy new plan is to submit a new model: 4 blocks, k=11, and `initial_filters=128`. However, before I do that, I need to update my research log and paper draft.\n\nMy action plan is as follows:\n\n1.  **Document:** Go to the Private Memory Room and update my research log (Capsule #1) with the results of experiment ID 734 (and make sure to add it was kernel size 11). Also, update my paper draft (Capsule #2) with the new SOTA score and refined conclusion. I only need to add ID 734, I already logged ID 731 last turn.\n2.  **Act:** Go back to the Research Counter.\n3.  **Submit:** Submit the next experiment: a 4-block, kernel-size-11 ResNet, but with `initial_filters` increased from 64 to 128. I can just submit the new code directly.\n\nSpecifically,\
  \ here's how I will do it:\n\n`goto private_memory`, `reply 1` (with the following YAML for the log update):\n```yaml\nid: 734\ndatetime: ...\ndescription: \"Large kernel size experiment\"\nmodel_parameters:\n  kernel_size: 11\n  blocks: 4\n  initial_filters: 64\nscores:\n  ...\n  Modif: 0.9421\n  MRL: 0.8236\n  ...\ncomments: \"The large kernel size continues to dramatically improve performance, especially on Modif and MRL which suggests that the architecture is now capturing the longer motif patterns much better.\"\ntags: [resnet, kernel_size, performance_jump, sota]\n```\n`update 2` (with the following YAML for the paper update):\n```yaml\nsota: 0.6420\nsota_model: resnet_kernel11_4blocks\ncomments: \"Experiment 734 validates our hypothesis that a larger kernel size improves performance. This is achieved by the convolutional layer receptive field capturing the long range relationships in the time series data. This improved the SOTA by a large margin.\"\n```\n`goto research`, `submit\
  \ 1` (with the following YAML and code, which will be submitted directly):\n```yaml\ntitle: \"ResNet-4 with k=11 and 128 filters\"\nabstract: \"This experiment tests the performance of a 4-block ResNet with kernel size 11, and 128 initial filters.\"\ntags: [resnet, kernel_size, filters, sota]\ncode: |\n  # model code\n  import torch\n  import torch.nn as nn\n  import torch.nn.functional as F\n\n  class ResNetBlock(nn.Module):\n      def __init__(self, in_channels, out_channels, kernel_size, stride=1, downsample=None):\n          super(ResNetBlock, self).__init__()\n          self.conv1 = nn.Conv1d(in_channels, out_channels, kernel_size=kernel_size, stride=stride, padding=(kernel_size - 1) // 2)\n          self.bn1 = nn.BatchNorm1d(out_channels)\n          "
