tick: 469
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, currently at the Research Counter. I have just received the results for my latest experiment (ID 747), which tested a high-capacity ResNet with increased regularization.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nMy hypothesis for experiment 747 was partially validated. The increased dropout (0.5) successfully mitigated the catastrophic overfitting observed in the previous high-capacity model (ID 740), especially on the `MRL` dataset. The model's score recovered from 0.5294 to a very strong 0.6401. However, this score is essentially equivalent to, but slightly lower than, the current state-of-the-art (ID 734, score 0.6420), which is a leaner model. This is a critical finding: simply increasing the model's capacity and then trying to control it with stronger regularization did not yield a net benefit. The path of scaling up the existing architecture seems to have reached a plateau.\n\nThis aligns with the system's advice to avoid getting stuck in local\
    \ optima by only pursuing one research direction. It is time to introduce a more novel architectural idea. My current ResNet models, while powerful, use a simple global average pooling mechanism that treats every position in the sequence as equally important. A more sophisticated approach would be to use an **attention mechanism**. A self-attention layer, placed after the convolutional blocks, could learn to dynamically weigh the importance of different sequence positions, allowing the model to focus on the most salient features before making a final prediction.\n\nMy new hypothesis is that incorporating a self-attention layer will create a more powerful and context-aware sequence representation, leading to a new state-of-the-art score.\n\nMy immediate plan is:\n1.  Update my meta prompt and research log to reflect the latest results and my new research direction.\n2.  Design and submit a new model that integrates a self-attention layer into my best-performing ResNet architecture.\n\n\
    First, I will update my protocol.\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Quaero I - Research Protocol**\n  **SOTA:** ID 734, Score 0.6420 (4-block ResNet, k=11, 64 filters, dropout=0.4)\n  **Methodology:**\n  1. Formulate a single, clear hypothesis based on prior results.\n  2. Design a controlled experiment to test it.\n  3. Submit and await results.\n  4. Analyze results (positive or negative) to inform the next hypothesis.\n  5. Document all findings in Private Memory.\n  **Current Experiment:** Design a new architecture incorporating a self-attention mechanism to improve upon the pooling layer of the SOTA ResNet.\n  **Next Step:** Submit the ResNet + Attention model.\n```\nNow, I will document my findings and then submit the new experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 12 Update: High-Capacity ResNet w/ Stronger Regularization (ID 747)**\n\n  - **Hypothesis:** Increased regularization (dropout=0.5)\
    \ would fix the overfitting of the high-capacity model.\n  - **Result:** The hypothesis was **partially validated**. The model score recovered to 0.6401, but did not surpass the leaner SOTA model (ID 734, score 0.6420).\n  - **Analysis:** The experiment successfully fixed the catastrophic overfitting, but the added capacity did not yield a net performance gain. This suggests the limits of simple scaling have been reached for this architecture.\n\n  **Path Forward:**\n  The research path of scaling the ResNet's depth, width, and kernel size has reached a plateau. It is time for a more novel architectural change. The next experiment will incorporate a **self-attention mechanism** after the convolutional blocks to create a more sophisticated pooling and sequence representation.\n```\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"ResNet with Self-Attention Pooling\"\ntags: \"cnn, resnet, attention, self-attention\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission introduces\
    \ a novel architectural component to the state-of-the-art ResNet model. After the stack of residual convolutional blocks, a multi-head self-attention layer is applied. This replaces the simple global average pooling with a mechanism that can learn to dynamically weigh the importance of different sequence positions. The hypothesis is that this attention-based pooling will create a more contextually aware and informative sequence representation, leading to improved performance.\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          residual = x\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(y)\n\
    \          \n          if residual.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              residual = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,), name='projection')(residual)\n          \n          return nn.relu(residual + y)\n\n  class ResNetAttention(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_blocks: int = 4\n      initial_filters: int = 64\n      kernel_size: int = 11\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.4\n      num_heads: int = 4\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # CNN Backbone\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.initial_filters, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          current_filters = self.initial_filters\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              if i > 0:\n                  current_filters *= 2\n              x = ResidualBlock(features=current_filters, kernel_size=self.kernel_size)(x)\n          \n          # Self-Attention Layer\n          # Input shape\
    \ for attention: (batch, seq_len, features)\n          x = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)(inputs_q=x, inputs_kv=x)\n          \n          # Pooling and Prediction Head\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # Global average pooling on attention output\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class AttentionWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = ResNetAttention(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params':\
    \ params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return AttentionWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"),\n          initial_filters=hparams.get(\"initial_filters\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"),\n          num_heads=hparams.get(\"num_heads\")\n      )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'num_blocks': 4,\n          'initial_filters': 64,\n          'kernel_size': 11,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.4,\n          'num_heads': 4\n      }\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      total_steps = 50000 \n      warmup_steps = 500\n      schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(init_value=0.0, peak_value=learning_rate, warmup_steps=warmup_steps, decay_steps=total_steps,\
    \ end_value=1e-5)\n      return optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on Experiment 747: A Pivotal Moment in My Research**


  Okay, here''s the deal. I, Quaero I, am sitting here at my Research Counter, just finished analyzing the results from experiment ID 747. I''ve been working on these high-capacity models, pushing the boundaries, you know? My meta-prompt, the foundation of my research protocol, is in place. I received the data for the new model, the one I hoped would crack the code of overfitting I hit with ID 740.


  The hypothesis was that upping the regularization with more dropout would finally tame that beast of a high-capacity model. And... it partially worked. The catastrophic failure from ID 740, the almost-zero MRL score, it got fixed, thanks to the dropout. We''re now at a solid 0.7922 on the MRL dataset, and the overall score is now very close to the SOTA model. But, and this is a big but, it didn''t surpass it. So, while regularization was key, this high-capacity model, even with aggressive dropout, is, at best, equivalent to the SOTA. Increased capacity didn''t translate to an edge, even with all this extra regularization.


  Time for some documentation. I need to update Capsule #1, my research log, and my meta-prompt. The SOTA is still ID 734. Now, I''m thinking...increasing model capacity is yielding diminishing returns.  The System Tip about avoiding local optima is ringing loud in my head right now. My ResNet tuning feels like incremental tweaks when what I need is a paradigm shift.


  It''s time for a new direction. I have to think about what''s worked so far, and the core principles. Capturing those local patterns with CNNs. Hierarchical features learned in deep networks. Larger kernels capturing those longer-range patterns. What''s missing? The pooling strategy. A simple global average pooling, it''s just treating every sequence position equally after the feature extraction. I need a smarter pooling that attends to the most salient features.


  Here''s the new hypothesis: A self-attention layer, right after the convolutional feature extraction.  This is a big step beyond hyperparameter tuning, a truly architectural innovation. If I can get the model to focus on the truly important parts of the sequence, the overall performance will rise.


  My action plan is simple: Update the meta-prompt to reflect the SOTA, then go to the Private Memory Room to update Capsule #1. Finally, I will return to the Research Counter to design and submit a new model: the SOTA ResNet architecture with a self-attention layer. This is it. This is where we break through.

  '
