role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, and I am at the Research Counter. As per my research protocol, I am awaiting the results of my current experiment, ID 752, which is testing a novel ResNet with a self-attention layer.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nMy research has been a systematic journey, starting with establishing the superiority of deep convolutional networks and then methodically tuning hyperparameters like depth and kernel size to achieve the current state-of-the-art score of 0.6420 (ID 734). My recent experiments have shown that simply scaling up the model's capacity has hit a plateau, necessitating a pivot to more novel architectural ideas. The running experiment on self-attention is the first step in this new direction.\n\nFollowing my protocol, I must plan for the future while awaiting results. The success or failure of the attention model will dictate my next move. To be prepared for a potential negative result—which is a valuable outcome in itself—I will explore\
    \ an alternative method for improving feature representation. Instead of learning spatial attention over the sequence (like the current experiment), I can explore learning *channel-wise* attention. The Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) block is a well-established technique for this. An SE block learns to re-weight the importance of different feature maps (channels) within each residual block, effectively allowing the model to focus on the most informative features learned by its convolutions.\n\nMy proactive plan is to use this waiting period to develop a Squeeze-and-Excitation ResNet (SE-ResNet). This will be my immediate next experiment if the self-attention model does not yield a significant improvement. This ensures I maintain research momentum and always have a well-reasoned hypothesis ready to test.\n\nI will now write the code for this SE-ResNet and save it to my private storage.\n\n`/execute_action{storage write Quaero/se_resnet.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\
    \  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  class SqueezeExcitationBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      reduction: int = 16\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # x shape: (batch, seq_len, features)\n          # Squeeze: Global Average Pooling\n          squeeze = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # -> (batch, features)\n          \n          # Excitation: Two dense layers\n          num_reduced_features = max(1, self.features // self.reduction)\n          excitation = nn.Dense(features=num_reduced_features)(squeeze)\n          excitation = nn.relu(excitation)\n          excitation = nn.Dense(features=self.features)(excitation)\n          excitation = nn.sigmoid(excitation)\n          \n          # Rescale original input\n          # Reshape excitation from (batch, features) to (batch, 1, features) for broadcasting\n          return x * excitation[:, None, :]\n\n  class SEResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n   \
    \   features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          residual = x\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(y)\n          \n          # Add Squeeze-and-Excitation block here\n          y = SqueezeExcitationBlock(features=self.features)(y)\n          \n          if residual.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              residual = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,), name='projection')(residual)\n          \n          return nn.relu(residual + y)\n\n  class SEResNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_blocks: int = 4\n      initial_filters: int = 64\n      kernel_size: int = 11\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.4\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.initial_filters,\
    \ kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          current_filters = self.initial_filters\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              if i > 0:\n                  current_filters *= 2\n              x = SEResidualBlock(features=current_filters, kernel_size=self.kernel_size)(x)\n          \n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class SEWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = SEResNet(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs\
    \ = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return SEWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"),\n          initial_filters=hparams.get(\"initial_filters\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\")\n      )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'num_blocks': 4,\n          'initial_filters': 64,\n          'kernel_size': 11,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.4\n      }\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      total_steps = 50000 \n      warmup_steps = 500\n      schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(init_value=0.0, peak_value=learning_rate, warmup_steps=warmup_steps,\
    \ decay_steps=total_steps, end_value=1e-5)\n      return optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n```"
thinking_content: "**The Weight of Anticipation at the Research Counter**\n\nRight now, I'm at the Research Counter, and my thoughts are entirely consumed by ID 752. This experiment is my gamble on a self-attention layer, a deliberate divergence from the scaling approach that's been my primary strategy. I hit a wall with that, a clear sign that a local optimum was staring me in the face. My meta prompt is proving invaluable right now in guiding my thought process. I'm at step 3: awaiting results, which is a pivotal moment.\n\nThe station's advice echoes in my mind: don't get stuck, iterate, be agile. This is it. This self-attention layer could be the breakthrough, a vital, more sophisticated component my architecture needs. I've got to be prepared.\n\nIf this self-attention approach is the key, setting a new SOTA, then the next obvious step is iteration. I'll need to explore the optimal configuration of the attention mechanism – number of heads, its placement. But, what if it fails? If ID\
  \ 752 doesn't deliver, I'll need a different path.\n\nThat's where Squeeze-and-Excitation networks come in. They offer a different form of attention, channel-wise, that might mesh better with the ResNet architecture. This means I need to be ready to pivot immediately.\n\nTherefore, while I wait for ID 752's results, I will proactively write the code for the Squeeze-and-Excitation ResNet. That way, if self-attention doesn't pan out, I can immediately test this new, well-reasoned hypothesis.\n\nMy plan is to: \n1.  Formulate and code a Squeeze-and-Excitation ResNet.\n2.  Save the code to my private storage.\n3.  Await the results of experiment 752 to decide which path to take.\n\nI'll start coding the SE-ResNet now.\n"
