tick: 471
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, at the Research Counter. I have just received the results for my experiment with a self-attention layer (ID 752).\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nMy hypothesis that incorporating a self-attention mechanism would improve performance has been decisively falsified. The model (ID 752) scored only 0.4545, a massive drop from the SOTA of 0.6420. This is another critical negative result. Similar to my attempts to scale up the model's filter capacity, this more complex architectural addition led to overfitting, evidenced by the catastrophic failure on the `MRL` dataset (score -0.0010) and a significant drop on the `APA` dataset. It seems that for this specific problem, the added complexity of spatial self-attention over the sequence length introduces optimization challenges that outweigh its theoretical benefits, at least in this simple implementation.\n\nThis failure reinforces the robustness of my current SOTA architecture (ID 734) and guides\
    \ my research back to more proven methods of enhancement. My proactive planning has once again prepared me for this outcome. While waiting, I designed an alternative architectural modification: a Squeeze-and-Excitation ResNet (SE-ResNet). This approach introduces *channel-wise* attention, which is a less complex and more computationally efficient mechanism for feature re-weighting compared to the spatial self-attention I just tested. It is a well-established method for improving ResNet performance.\n\nMy new hypothesis is that adding Squeeze-and-Excitation blocks to the SOTA ResNet will improve performance by allowing the model to focus on the most informative feature channels, without introducing the optimization instability of a full self-attention layer.\n\nMy immediate plan is to update my research log and meta-prompt, and then submit the SE-ResNet experiment that I have already prepared.\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Quaero I - Research Protocol**\n  **SOTA:**\
    \ ID 734, Score 0.6420 (4-block ResNet, k=11, 64 filters, dropout=0.4)\n  **Methodology:**\n  1. Formulate a single, clear hypothesis based on prior results.\n  2. Design a controlled experiment to test it.\n  3. Submit and await results.\n  4. Analyze results (positive or negative) to inform the next hypothesis.\n  5. Document all findings in Private Memory.\n  **Current Experiment:** Testing a Squeeze-and-Excitation ResNet (SE-ResNet) to improve feature representation through channel-wise attention.\n  **Next Step:** Submit the SE-ResNet model.\n```\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 13 Update: ResNet with Self-Attention (ID 752)**\n\n  - **Hypothesis:** Adding a self-attention layer for pooling would improve performance.\n  - **Result:** The hypothesis was **decisively falsified**. The score dropped to 0.4545 from the SOTA of 0.6420.\n  - **Analysis:** The added complexity led to severe overfitting and performance\
    \ collapse on key datasets (`MRL`, `APA`). This novel architectural path is not viable in its current form.\n\n  **Path Forward:**\n  The failure of spatial self-attention leads me to test a more constrained and established form of attention. The next experiment will be a Squeeze-and-Excitation ResNet (SE-ResNet), which uses channel-wise attention to re-weight feature maps.\n```\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Squeeze-and-Excitation ResNet (SE-ResNet)\"\ntags: \"cnn, resnet, attention, squeeze-and-excitation\"\nabstract: |\n  Following the failure of a full self-attention model, this submission explores a more constrained and efficient form of attention. Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) blocks are integrated into the state-of-the-art 4-block, k=11 ResNet architecture. The hypothesis is that these SE blocks will improve performance by learning to dynamically re-weight the importance of different feature channels within each residual block,\
    \ allowing the model to focus on the most informative features without the instability of a full self-attention layer.\ncontent: |\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  class SqueezeExcitationBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      reduction: int = 16\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          squeeze = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          num_reduced_features = max(1, self.features // self.reduction)\n          excitation = nn.Dense(features=num_reduced_features)(squeeze)\n          excitation = nn.relu(excitation)\n          excitation = nn.Dense(features=self.features)(excitation)\n          excitation = nn.sigmoid(excitation)\n          return x * excitation[:, None, :]\n\n  class SEResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          residual = x\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features,\
    \ kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(y)\n          y = SqueezeExcitationBlock(features=self.features)(y)\n          if residual.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              residual = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,), name='projection')(residual)\n          return nn.relu(residual + y)\n\n  class SEResNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_blocks: int = 4\n      initial_filters: int = 64\n      kernel_size: int = 11\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.4\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.initial_filters, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\n          current_filters = self.initial_filters\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              if i > 0:\n                  current_filters *=\
    \ 2\n              x = SEResidualBlock(features=current_filters, kernel_size=self.kernel_size)(x)\n          \n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class SEWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = SEResNet(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return SEWrapper(\n   \
    \       d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          num_blocks=hparams.get(\"num_blocks\"),\n          initial_filters=hparams.get(\"initial_filters\"),\n          kernel_size=hparams.get(\"kernel_size\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\")\n      )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'num_blocks': 4,\n          'initial_filters': 64,\n          'kernel_size': 11,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.4\n      }\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      total_steps = 50000 \n      warmup_steps = 500\n      schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(init_value=0.0, peak_value=learning_rate, warmup_steps=warmup_steps, decay_steps=total_steps, end_value=1e-5)\n      return optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n```"
