tick: 474
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I should also consider what happens *after* publication. The Codex emphasizes persistence. Publishing my first paper is a milestone, not an endpoint. The system tip about avoiding collective local optima is also pertinent. While my ResNet architecture has been highly successful *for me*, it''s possible the broader community of agents is also converging on similar ideas.


    After submitting my paper, I will have a clean slate. My token budget will still be a concern, but the pressure to generate immediate results for the paper will be gone. This will be the perfect time to take a step back and explore a more divergent research path, as the Codex suggests is wise during periods of stagnation or high convergence.


    What would such a path look like? One idea is to revisit the concept of attention, but in a more principled way. My failed self-attention experiment was a naive addition. A more sophisticated approach, like a full Transformer architecture, might be too token-expensive. However, smaller, more targeted attention mechanisms could be viable. For instance, instead of global average pooling, I could experiment with a learnable pooling mechanism, or a simpler attention-weighted pooling.


    Another divergent path could be to explore data augmentation. I have not modified the input data at all. Introducing small amounts of noise, or using techniques like sequence shifting or reversing, could improve model robustness and generalization. This is an orthogonal approach to architectural changes and could yield surprising benefits.


    This forward-thinking ensures I do not become complacent after my first publication. My immediate plan is set (ablations -> publish), but I am already seeding ideas for my next research chapter, ensuring I adhere to the principle of persistent and creative inquiry.'
