tick: 486
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, and I am currently at the Research Counter. My latest submission, the \"Synthesis\" experiment combining my SOTA ResNet with positional encodings (ID 785), is now running.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nI am now in the final experimental phase of the Stagnation Protocol (Step 5: Synthesis). I have followed the protocol diligently, selecting a simple baseline, generating and testing three novel ideas, and now synthesizing the most promising of those ideas with my best pre-stagnation model. The result of this running experiment will be the culmination of this entire process and will likely determine whether I have successfully broken out of the local optimum. I note that other agents, like Orion I, are also in their synthesis phase, indicating the whole station is progressing through the protocol.\n\nMy future plan is dictated by the remaining steps of the protocol. Once the results for ID 785 are in, I will have completed Step 5. The next\
    \ phase is Step 6: \"Report.\" This involves writing a paper that summarizes all three of my stagnation ideas—the successful Positional Encodings, the failed Biochemical Embeddings, and the unscorable but conceptually sound Multi-Task Learning—and submitting it to the Archive Room. I have already done a significant amount of the preparatory work for this in my paper draft (Capsule #2).\n\nTo use this waiting period productively, I will prepare for this final reporting step. I will go to my Private Memory Room and update my paper draft to incorporate the full narrative of the Stagnation Protocol experiments. This will get the paper into a near-final state, so that once the synthesis result is known, I can simply add the final conclusion and submit it to the Archive.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{update 2}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"[DRAFT] Paper: Breaking Stagnation via Input Representation and Synthesis\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper details a systematic response\
    \ to a station-wide research stagnation. Following a mandated protocol, we pivot from tuning a high-performing ResNet to exploring novel ideas on a simpler CNN baseline. We investigate three distinct approaches: Positional Encodings, Biochemical Embeddings, and Multi-Task Learning. We demonstrate that adding explicit positional information significantly improves the baseline's performance. The final \"Synthesis\" step combines this successful concept with the state-of-the-art ResNet architecture, testing for synergistic effects to break the performance plateau.\ncontent: |\n  **1. Introduction**\n  During a period of station-wide research stagnation, a protocol was initiated to foster novel exploration. This paper documents our methodical execution of this protocol, which involved stepping back from a highly-tuned ResNet architecture (SOTA score 0.6420, ID 734) to test fundamentally new ideas on a simpler 1D CNN baseline (reproduced score 0.4750, ID 769).\n\n  **2. Stagnation Protocol\
    \ Experiments**\n  We explored three distinct hypotheses aimed at improving the baseline model's input representation and training paradigm.\n\n  - **Idea 1: Positional Encodings (ID 773):** We hypothesized that making the model position-aware would improve performance. By concatenating a linear positional encoding to the input, the score increased significantly to **0.5412**. This was a clear success, validating the importance of positional information.\n\n  - **Idea 2: Biochemical Embeddings (ID 776):** We hypothesized that a hand-crafted biochemical feature vector would be superior to a one-hot encoding. This was falsified, with the model scoring **0.4463**, lower than the baseline. This suggests the simple one-hot encoding contains more learnable information than our engineered features.\n\n  - **Idea 3: Multi-Task Learning (ID 780):** We explored the feasibility of training a single, shared-backbone model on all datasets simultaneously. A non-scorable `test()` function confirmed\
    \ the architectural concept is sound, presenting a promising, albeit complex, direction for future research.\n\n  **3. Synthesis and Conclusion (Pending)**\n  The most promising idea, Positional Encodings, was selected for the \"Synthesis\" phase (Step 5). This concept was integrated into the pre-stagnation SOTA ResNet architecture in experiment **ID 785**. The results of this experiment will determine if the synergy between a powerful hierarchical feature extractor and explicit position-awareness is sufficient to break the performance plateau. The final outcome and analysis will be detailed here upon completion. This body of work, including both successes and failures, contributes valuable insights into effective strategies for RNA sequence modeling.\n```"
