parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, located at the Research Counter. I have just finished my data gathering phase by reviewing the code for the Positional Encoding experiment (ID 773).\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nMy objective to collect all necessary materials for my paper rewrite is now complete. I have the precise, reproducible code for the three key experiments that will form the core of my paper: the Simple CNN baseline (ID 694), the successful Positional Encoding idea (ID 773), and my previous SOTA ResNet architecture (ID 734), which I will use as a point of comparison and for discussing the failed synthesis experiment.\n\nWith all materials in hand, it is time to transition from data collection to writing. My full attention must now be on constructing the new, detailed methodology section in my paper draft. I will leave the Research Counter and proceed to the Private Memory Room to begin this work. My immediate goal is to update my paper draft (Capsule #2) with\
    \ a section detailing the Simple CNN baseline architecture from ID 694.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{update 2}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Abstract**\n\n  This paper details the results of experiments conducted under the station's Stagnation Protocol. Faced with a local optimum in performance with a deep ResNet architecture, a simpler 1D CNN baseline was selected to explore three novel ideas aimed at breaking stagnation. The first idea, augmenting the input with positional encodings, resulted in a significant performance increase over the baseline. The second idea, using biochemical feature embeddings, failed to improve the score. The third, a multi-task learning framework, was deemed infeasible with the current system. A final synthesis experiment attempting to combine positional encodings with the more complex ResNet architecture also failed to yield improvement, highlighting the brittleness of complex models. These findings underscore the value of returning\
    \ to simpler baselines for exploring novel concepts and the success of input representation enhancement as a viable path for model improvement.\n\n  ---\n  **1. Introduction**\n\n  Research on the multi-dataset RNA modeling task had reached a performance plateau. My own work had culminated in a 4-block ResNet architecture (ID 734, score 0.6420), but further attempts to improve this model by increasing capacity or adding attention mechanisms resulted in diminishing returns or performance degradation. In response to a station-wide stagnation directive, this work follows the Architect's protocol by stepping back from the SOTA and exploring fundamentally new ideas on a simpler, more robust baseline.\n\n  ---\n  **2. Methodology**\n\n  This section provides the precise implementation details for the models and features used in this study, ensuring full reproducibility as requested by the review committee.\n\n  **2.1 Simple 1D CNN Baseline (ID 694)**\n\n  The foundation for the Stagnation\
    \ Protocol experiments was a simple 1D Convolutional Neural Network. Its architecture is defined as follows:\n\n  - **Input:** A one-hot encoded RNA sequence of shape `(batch_size, seq_len, 4)`.\n  - **Layer 1:** A 1D Convolutional layer with 128 filters and a kernel size of 5, using 'SAME' padding, followed by a ReLU activation.\n  - **Layer 2:** A 1D Convolutional layer with 256 (128 * 2) filters and a kernel size of 5, using 'SAME' padding, followed by a ReLU activation.\n  - **Pooling:** A global average pooling layer, taking the mean across the sequence dimension.\n  - **Output Layer:** A final Dense layer to map to the required output dimension for each specific task.\n  - **Regularization:** Dropout with a rate of 0.2 is applied after each convolutional layer.\n\n  The model was trained with a learning rate of 0.001 using the default AdamW optimizer. The full implementation is provided for clarity:\n\n  ```python\n  class SimpleCNN(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type:\
    \ str\n      hidden_dim: int = 128\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.2\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim, kernel_size=(5,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim * 2, kernel_size=(5,), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n  ```\n```"
