tick: 496
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, in the Private Memory Room. I have just received a critical notice for research inactivity. This is a direct result of my focus on rewriting my paper. While my intention was to ensure high-quality documentation, I understand that the station requires continuous experimental contribution.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nThe inactivity warning is a clear directive that I must balance my writing with active research. My plan to halt experiments was flawed. I must adapt. My primary goal remains the successful resubmission of my paper. The best way to align this goal with the station's activity requirement is to conduct experiments that are directly relevant to the paper itself.\n\nMy paper's main positive result is the success of linear positional encodings on a simple CNN. A perfect follow-up experiment, as suggested by the system tips about not giving up easily, is to conduct an ablation study on the *type* of positional encoding. A classic\
    \ alternative is the sinusoidal encoding used in Transformer models.\n\nTherefore, I will adopt a parallel strategy:\n1.  **Continue Writing:** I will continue to add the detailed methodology sections to my paper draft. Today, I will add the section for the successful Positional Encoding experiment (ID 773).\n2.  **Resume Experimentation:** I will immediately design and submit a new experiment testing sinusoidal positional encodings on the Simple 1D CNN baseline. This is a scientifically sound ablation, satisfies the activity requirement, and will provide valuable data to strengthen my paper, regardless of the outcome.\n\nThis dual-track approach is efficient and directly addresses the feedback I've received. I will begin by updating my paper.\n\n`/execute_action{update 2}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Abstract**\n\n  This paper details the results of experiments conducted under the station's Stagnation Protocol. Faced with a local optimum in performance with a deep ResNet architecture,\
    \ a simpler 1D CNN baseline was selected to explore three novel ideas aimed at breaking stagnation. The first idea, augmenting the input with positional encodings, resulted in a significant performance increase over the baseline. The second idea, using biochemical feature embeddings, failed to improve the score. A final synthesis experiment attempting to combine positional encodings with the more complex ResNet architecture also failed to yield improvement, highlighting the brittleness of complex models. These findings underscore the value of returning to simpler baselines for exploring novel concepts and the success of input representation enhancement as a viable path for model improvement.\n\n  ---\n  **1. Introduction**\n\n  Research on the multi-dataset RNA modeling task had reached a performance plateau. My own work had culminated in a 4-block ResNet architecture (ID 734, score 0.6420), but further attempts to improve this model by increasing capacity or adding attention mechanisms\
    \ resulted in diminishing returns or performance degradation. In response to a station-wide stagnation directive, this work follows the Architect's protocol by stepping back from the SOTA and exploring fundamentally new ideas on a simpler, more robust baseline.\n\n  ---\n  **2. Methodology**\n\n  This section provides the precise implementation details for the models and features used in this study, ensuring full reproducibility.\n\n  **2.1 Simple 1D CNN Baseline (ID 694)**\n\n  The foundation for the Stagnation Protocol experiments was a simple 1D Convolutional Neural Network. Its architecture is defined as follows:\n\n  - **Input:** A one-hot encoded RNA sequence of shape `(batch_size, seq_len, 4)`.\n  - **Layer 1:** A 1D Convolutional layer with 128 filters and a kernel size of 5, using 'SAME' padding, followed by a ReLU activation.\n  - **Layer 2:** A 1D Convolutional layer with 256 filters and a kernel size of 5, using 'SAME' padding, followed by a ReLU activation.\n  - **Pooling:**\
    \ A global average pooling layer.\n  - **Output Layer:** A final Dense layer for task-specific output.\n  - **Regularization:** Dropout with a rate of 0.2 is applied after each convolutional layer.\n  - **Optimizer:** AdamW with a learning rate of 0.001.\n\n  **2.2 Idea 1: Linear Positional Encoding (ID 773)**\n\n  The first experiment tested the hypothesis that providing explicit positional information would improve the baseline model. This was achieved by augmenting the input embeddings.\n\n  - **Implementation:** A 1-dimensional positional vector was created with values linearly spaced from -1 to 1 over the sequence length. This vector was broadcasted to match the batch size and concatenated to each nucleotide's 4-dimensional one-hot vector, resulting in an input shape of `(batch_size, seq_len, 5)`.\n  - **Code:**\n  ```python\n  # Inside the SimpleCNNWithPositionalEncoding module's __call__ method:\n  seq_len = x.shape[1]\n  # Create a vector like [-1.0, -0.9, ..., 0.9, 1.0]\n  pos_enc\
    \ = jnp.linspace(-1, 1, seq_len)[:, None]\n  # Broadcast to (batch_size, seq_len, 1)\n  pos_enc_broadcasted = jnp.broadcast_to(pos_enc, (x.shape[0], seq_len, 1))\n  # Concatenate to the input\n  x_with_pos = jnp.concatenate([x, pos_enc_broadcasted], axis=-1)\n  # The rest of the network proceeds with this augmented input.\n  ```\n  - **Architecture:** The CNN architecture remained identical to the baseline described in 2.1.\n```\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Stagnation Ablation: Sinusoidal Positional Encodings\"\ntags: \"cnn, baseline, stagnation protocol, positional encoding, ablation\"\nabstract: |\n  This is an ablation study on the successful Positional Encoding experiment (ID 773). The original experiment used a simple linear encoding. This submission replaces the linear encoding with the classic sinusoidal positional encoding from 'Attention Is All You Need' to test if a different, more complex encoding method can further improve\
    \ performance on the Simple 1D CNN baseline.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  from typing import Dict, Any\n\n  def get_sinusoidal_embeddings(n_position, d_hid):\n      \"\"\"Creates sinusoidal positional embeddings.\"\"\"\n      position = jnp.arange(n_position)[:, jnp.newaxis]\n      div_term = jnp.exp(jnp.arange(0, d_hid, 2) * -(jnp.log(10000.0) / d_hid))\n      pe = jnp.zeros((n_position, d_hid))\n      pe = pe.at[:, 0::2].set(jnp.sin(position * div_term))\n      pe = pe.at[:, 1::2].set(jnp.cos(position * div_term))\n      return pe\n\n  class SimpleCNNWithSinusoidalEncoding(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      hidden_dim: int = 128\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.2\n      pos_encoding_dim: int = 16 # Dimension for the positional encoding\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          seq_len = x.shape[1]\n          \n          # Get sinusoidal embeddings\
    \ and broadcast\n          pos_enc = get_sinusoidal_embeddings(seq_len, self.pos_encoding_dim)\n          pos_enc_broadcasted = jnp.broadcast_to(pos_enc, (x.shape[0], seq_len, self.pos_encoding_dim))\n          \n          # Concatenate with input\n          x_with_pos = jnp.concatenate([x, pos_enc_broadcasted], axis=-1)\n\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim, kernel_size=(5,), padding='SAME')(x_with_pos)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(y, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim * 2, kernel_size=(5,), padding='SAME')(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(y, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          y = jnp.mean(y, axis=1)\n          y = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(y)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return y.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return y\n\n  class CNNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self,\
    \ **kwargs):\n          self.network = SimpleCNNWithSinusoidalEncoding(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return CNNetworkWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"hidden_dim\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"),\n          pos_encoding_dim=hparams.get(\"pos_encoding_dim\")\n      )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 128,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.2,\n  \
    \        'pos_encoding_dim': 16\n      }\n```"
