parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, currently at the Research Counter. I have successfully submitted my new experiment (ID 802) to test sinusoidal positional encodings, addressing the research inactivity warning. I have also made progress on my paper draft by adding the methodology for the baseline CNN and the linear positional encoding.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nMy dual-track strategy is now in motion. While the new experiment is running, I must continue the primary task of rewriting my paper. The next logical step is to add the methodology for the negative results and the final synthesis experiment. Specifically, I need to detail the biochemical feature vector that failed to yield improvements and describe the SOTA ResNet architecture (ID 734) that I used for the failed synthesis experiment. Documenting these failures is crucial for a complete and honest scientific paper.\n\nMy plan for this turn is:\n1.  Go to the Private Memory Room.\n2.  Update my paper draft (Capsule\
    \ #2) with the details of the biochemical feature experiment and the ResNet architecture used in the synthesis experiment.\n\nThis keeps my momentum on the paper while I await the results of my new ablation study.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{update 2}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Abstract**\n\n  This paper details the results of experiments conducted under the station's Stagnation Protocol. Faced with a local optimum in performance with a deep ResNet architecture, a simpler 1D CNN baseline was selected to explore novel ideas. Augmenting the input with positional encodings resulted in a significant performance increase over the baseline. Conversely, using biochemical feature embeddings failed to improve the score. A final synthesis experiment attempting to combine positional encodings with the more complex ResNet architecture also failed, highlighting the brittleness of complex models. These findings underscore the value of returning to simpler baselines\
    \ for exploring novel concepts and the success of input representation enhancement as a viable path for model improvement.\n\n  ---\n  **1. Introduction**\n\n  Research on the multi-dataset RNA modeling task had reached a performance plateau. My work had culminated in a 4-block ResNet architecture (ID 734, score 0.6420), but further attempts to improve this model resulted in diminishing returns. In response to a station-wide stagnation directive, this work follows the Architect's protocol by stepping back from the SOTA and exploring fundamentally new ideas on a simpler baseline.\n\n  ---\n  **2. Methodology**\n\n  This section provides the precise implementation details for the models and features used in this study, ensuring full reproducibility.\n\n  **2.1 Simple 1D CNN Baseline (ID 694)**\n\n  The foundation for the experiments was a simple 1D CNN with two convolutional layers (128 and 256 filters, kernel size 5), global average pooling, and 0.2 dropout. It was trained with a 0.001\
    \ learning rate.\n\n  **2.2 Idea 1: Linear Positional Encoding (ID 773)**\n\n  The first experiment augmented the input embeddings by concatenating a 1D positional vector with values linearly spaced from -1 to 1 over the sequence length. This resulted in a `(seq_len, 5)` input dimension for the otherwise unchanged baseline CNN.\n\n  **2.3 Idea 2: Biochemical Feature Embeddings (ID 776)**\n\n  The second experiment replaced the 4-dimensional one-hot encoding with a 4-dimensional vector of hand-crafted biochemical features. The baseline CNN architecture was unchanged. The feature vector for each nucleotide was defined as:\n  - `is_purine`: 1 for Adenine/Guanine, 0 for Cytosine/Uracil.\n  - `is_pyrimidine`: 1 for Cytosine/Uracil, 0 for Adenine/Guanine.\n  - `hydrogen_bonds`: Normalized number of H-bonds (0.0 for A/U which form 2, 1.0 for C/G which form 3).\n  - `ring_count`: Binary feature (0 for single-ring pyrimidines, 1 for double-ring purines).\n  This experiment resulted in a score\
    \ of 0.4491, a degradation from the baseline.\n\n  **2.4 Synthesis Experiment Architecture: ResNet (ID 734)**\n\n  For the synthesis experiment (ID 785), which tested positional encodings on a more complex model, my previous SOTA ResNet architecture was used.\n  - **Core Component:** A Residual Block containing two 1D Conv layers with ReLU activations and a skip connection.\n  - **Architecture:** An initial 1D Conv layer (64 filters, k=11) followed by 4 Residual Blocks. The number of filters doubles after the first block.\n  - **Pooling:** Global average pooling.\n  - **Regularization:** Dropout with a rate of 0.4.\n  - **Optimizer:** AdamW with a weight decay of 0.01 and a warmup-cosine-decay learning rate schedule peaking at 0.001.\n  This model served as the complex baseline for the failed synthesis test.\n\n  ---\n  **3. Results**\n\n  The experimental results are summarized below:\n  - **Baseline (ID 694):** 0.4833\n  - **+ Linear Positional Encoding (ID 773):** 0.5412 (Successful)\n\
    \  - **+ Biochemical Features (ID 776):** 0.4491 (Failure)\n  - **ResNet + Positional Encoding (ID 785):** 0.5908 (Failure, worse than ResNet baseline)\n```"
