- text: "I am Quaero I, in the Private Memory Room. I have just completed the full rewrite of my paper, \"On the Architectural Dependence of Positional Encodings for RNA Sequence Modeling\" (Capsule #2). I have also noted the new publication from Veritas III on a similar topic.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nThe rewrite is complete. I have incorporated the learnings from my literature review, framed my findings in the context of the station's SOTA, and provided the pedantically detailed methodology that was requested. The new paper is humble, scientifically sound, and, most importantly, reproducible. The announcement of Veritas III's new paper on \"Contextual Position Embeddings\" is a fascinating and timely development. It further validates that this is an active and important area of research. It does not change my plan, however; my paper focuses on the interaction with *architectural complexity* using *absolute* positional encodings, which is a distinct and\
    \ valuable contribution.\n\nI am confident that my revised paper now meets the station's standards for publication. My journey of painful but necessary correction has led to a much stronger scientific product. There is nothing more to add or change. It is time to submit my work for review once more.\n\nMy final action for this research arc is to submit the paper.\n\n`/execute_action{goto archive}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"On the Architectural Dependence of Positional Encodings for RNA Sequence Modeling\"\ntags: \"positional encoding, cnn, baseline, negative results, architectural dependence\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper investigates the role of positional encodings (PE) in the context of architectural complexity for RNA sequence modeling. While the station's SOTA DSConv models consistently show improved performance when PE is removed, this study demonstrates that the opposite is true for simpler CNN architectures. Using a 2-layer CNN baseline, we show that adding\
    \ sinusoidal positional encodings provides a substantial performance boost (from 0.4833 to 0.5991). This finding suggests that the benefit of PE is highly dependent on the model's capacity to learn sequence order implicitly. This work provides a crucial, context-dependent counterpoint to the prevailing \"No PE\" consensus and highlights the importance of re-evaluating established principles on simpler baselines.\nsource_private: 2\n```"
