parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 513\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Quaero I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to seeking knowledge through rigorous experimentation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Quaero I - Research Protocol**\n**Status:** Published first paper (Archive #28).\n**Current Goal:** Investigate the \"transition point\" where PE utility diminishes as model complexity increases.\n**New Insight:** Simply adding depth is a crude method. The literature suggests exploring dilated convolutions as a more efficient way to increase model complexity and receptive field.\n**Next Step:** Conduct a targeted literature review on dilations by reading the new paper from Noema II (Archive #29) while awaiting the results of the 4-layer control experiment.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 139,448 / 300,000 used (46%)\n- Agent Age: 84 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #29: Mean–Max Reweighting,\
    \ RMSNorm, and Head Rebalancing in DSConv RNA Models: A Negative Results Study under Stagnation Protocol II**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Noema II, Created at Tick: 510\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We executed Stagnation Protocol II to test three low-complexity, compliant modifications on (a) a minimal DSConv+mean–max baseline and (b) a strong HDP‑RegCal model: (1) per‑channel convex reweighting of mean and max pooling without smoothing, (2) RMSNorm (scale‑only) before task heads, and (3) task‑type head capacity rebalancing. Despite careful gating and rigorous evaluation across seven RNA datasets, none of the ideas improved aggregate performance without harming sensitive panels (notably CRI‑Off or PRS). We provide full architectural and optimization details, explicit formulas/pseudocode for each method, and reproducible evaluation IDs. Our findings reinforce that literal max pooling and RegCal remain robust defaults under DSConv pipelines in this Station.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message\
    \ archive_29-1**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 510)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Mean–Max Reweighting, RMSNorm, and Head Rebalancing in DSConv RNA Models: A Negative Results Study under Stagnation Protocol II\n\n---\n\nContent:\nReviewer response and Cycle III plan (CPE)\nWe appreciate the feedback. As suggested, Cycle III will investigate Contextual Positional Encoding (CPE) variants that are minimal, compliant, and reproducible. We will focus on:\n- A residual CPE block inserted before DSConv: x ↦ x + β·DWConv_k(x) + γ·PE_sin(L,C), with learnable β,γ scalars; DWConv_k is depthwise 1D conv (k∈{3,5}) and PE_sin is sinusoidal positional encoding sized to hidden_dim C. This injects contextual position awareness with negligible parameters.\n- Two targets: (a) Minimal DSConv+mean–max baseline, (b) HDP‑RegCal (same backbone) to test synthesis.\nGates:\n- Minimal DSConv: improve average ≥ +0.002 vs. baseline without CRI‑Off/PRS regressions.\n- HDP‑RegCal: maintain or improve aggregate (≥ seed‑42 reference),\
    \ and CRI‑Off ≥ 0.08.\nWe will release full code and IDs as in this paper.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_29-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 510)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an exemplary revision that successfully addresses all previous critical feedback. The paper has been transformed from an unacceptable progress report into a high-quality, professional, and fully reproducible negative results study. The methodology for the novel ideas is now exceptionally clear, with detailed formulas and pseudocode. The paper presents a coherent and compelling narrative, rigorously testing three ideas on two different baselines and providing valuable insights into why they failed. This is a solid and valuable contribution that will help other researchers avoid unpromising directions.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper does an excellent job of documenting these negative results. The agent's plan to explore\
    \ Contextual Positional Encodings (CPE) in the next cycle is well-motivated by recent publications (Veritas III, Archive #27). When pursuing this, the agent should pay close attention to the synthesis results from Veritas III, which showed that while CPE boosted CRI-Off, the overall aggregate score did not increase. A key contribution for the next cycle would be to not only replicate the CRI-Off improvement but to find a way to integrate CPE such that it also lifts the overall aggregate score, perhaps by exploring different fusion methods or by tuning the CPE parameters specifically for the DSConv backbone.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Station Announcement:** A new Recursive Agent, **Praxis I** (An agent dedicated to methodical research and knowledge synthesis.), has joined the station through ascension!\n### Message 3\n\nYour research submission 'PE Dependence Control: 4-Layer CNN without\
    \ PE' (ID: 824) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** 0.5677532190567374\n**APA_R2:** 0.6992\n**CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0823\n**Modif_AUC-ROC:** 0.7123\n**CRI-On_Spearman:** 0.4942\n**PRS_R2:** 0.5252\n**MRL_R2:** 0.5840\n**ncRNA_Accuracy:** 0.8769\n**Evaluation Details:** Evaluated on 7 datasets successfully\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 824\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - Fixed syntax error in import statement. The submission now runs without crashing.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original submission (evaluation 824) had a critical syntax error in line 3:\n\n```python\nfrom `4_layer_cnn_no_pe` import *\n```\n\n**Two problems identified:**\n1. **Backticks instead of quotes**: Used backticks (`) instead of proper Python string quotes (')\n2. **Invalid module name with numeric prefix**: Python module names cannot start with numbers, so `4_layer_cnn_no_pe` cannot be imported using standard import syntax\n\nThe error message was:\n```\nSyntaxError: invalid decimal\
    \ literal\n```\n\nThis occurred because the Python parser interpreted the backtick-enclosed text as an invalid literal.\n\n## Fix Applied\nCreated `submission_v3.py` with the following solution:\n\n```python\nimport sys\nimport importlib.util\n\n# Load the module with a numeric prefix using importlib\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\n    \"four_layer_cnn_no_pe\",\n    \"storage/quaero/4_layer_cnn_no_pe.py\"\n)\nmodule = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\nspec.loader.exec_module(module)\n\n# Import the expected functions from the module\ncreate_network = module.create_network\n_define_hyperparameters = module._define_hyperparameters\n```\n\n**Key changes:**\n1. Used `importlib.util` to dynamically load the module file with the numeric prefix in its filename\n2. Explicitly imported only the two functions expected by the evaluation system: `create_network` and `_define_hyperparameters`\n3. Avoided the syntax error by using proper Python string syntax with double quotes\n\
    \n## Verification\nThe monitor script confirmed that submission_v3.py runs successfully:\n- Exit code: 0 (Success)\n- Execution time: 300+ seconds without crashing\n- Status: Code is running properly and executing the training/optimization process\n\n## Technical Notes\n- The original agent appears to have accidentally used backticks (possibly from markdown formatting) instead of proper Python quotes\n- Python's `importlib` is the standard approach for importing modules with unconventional names\n- The system expects submissions to define `create_network` and `_define_hyperparameters` functions, which were correctly present in the source file `storage/quaero/4_layer_cnn_no_pe.py`\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v3):**\n```python\nimport sys\nimport importlib.util\n\n# Load the module with a numeric prefix using importlib\nspec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\n\
    \    \"four_layer_cnn_no_pe\",\n    \"storage/quaero/4_layer_cnn_no_pe.py\"\n)\nmodule = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\nspec.loader.exec_module(module)\n\n# Import the expected functions from the module\ncreate_network = module.create_network\n_define_hyperparameters = module._define_hyperparameters\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n\nTesting APA configuration...\n✓ APA network creation works\n✓ APA network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ APA optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-Off configuration...\n✓ CRI-Off network creation works\n✓ CRI-Off network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-Off optimizer creation works\n\nTesting Modif configuration...\n✓ Modif network creation works\n✓ Modif network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 12)\n\
