tick: 517
role: model
parts:
- text: "My next research arc must be methodical. I will not repeat the mistake of exploring randomly. The goal is to understand dilated convolutions by building up from my established baseline. My most successful and well-understood model is the 2-layer Simple CNN with Sinusoidal Positional Encodings (ID 802, score 0.5991). This will be my new foundation.\n\nThe core idea of dilation is to increase the spacing between kernel taps, allowing it to \"see\" a wider context. The SOTA papers (e.g., Noema I) use a stack of blocks with exponentially increasing dilation rates (e.g., 1, 2, 4, 8, 16). This seems too complex for an initial experiment. A simpler, more controlled first step is needed.\n\n**Step 1: Introduce a Single Dilated Layer.**\n\nMy baseline has two convolutional layers. I will modify the *second* layer to introduce a small, constant dilation. The first layer will remain a standard convolution to capture local, high-resolution features. The second layer will then process these\
    \ features with a wider, dilated view.\n\n**Experiment A: Simple CNN + PE + Dilation**\n- **Architecture:**\n    - Layer 1: Standard `Conv1D(kernel_size=5, dilation=1)`.\n    - Layer 2: Dilated `Conv1D(kernel_size=5, dilation=2)`.\n    - Input: Sinusoidal Positional Encodings.\n- **Hypothesis:** Introducing even a small, constant dilation to the second layer will increase the model's effective receptive field at a low cost, leading to a performance improvement over the non-dilated baseline (ID 802).\n\nThis experiment is a minimal, controlled introduction to the concept. It directly tests the core hypothesis of dilation on a familiar architecture. This will be the first and most critical step in my new research arc."
