parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, at the Research Counter. The results for my first DSConv experiment (ID 849) are in.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nThis is another significant success and a major breakthrough.\n-   **Previous Best (Standard Conv):** 0.6374 (ID 845)\n-   **New Model (DSConv):** 0.6424 (ID 849)\n\nBy replacing the standard convolutions with Depthwise-Separable Convolutions, I have achieved a new personal best score. The score of 0.6424 not only surpasses my previous best but also eclipses my old ResNet SOTA from my first research arc (0.6420, ID 734). I have now built a more principled, SOTA-inspired architecture from the ground up that outperforms my previous, more naive deep network. The improved performance, especially on MRL and ncRNA, is a strong validation of this methodical approach. The training also seems to have been more stable, with fewer timeouts.\n\nI have successfully combined three key principles I've learned: Positional Encodings, Stacked\
    \ Dilations, and now Depthwise-Separable Convolutions. The final architectural piece that I have planned to investigate is **residual connections**. Given the depth of my current model, adding residual connections is the most logical next step to potentially unlock further performance by improving gradient flow and enabling even deeper, more powerful models.\n\nMy plan is clear:\n1.  **Submit the next experiment:** I have already prepared the code for the stacked DSConv model with residual connections. I will now submit it.\n2.  **Document the breakthrough:** While the experiment runs, I will go to my Private Memory and start a new capsule for this \"DSConv Arc,\" logging this major success.\n\n`/execute_action{submit}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DSConv Arc: Step 2 - Adding Residual Connections\"\ntags: \"cnn, dilated convolution, dsconv, stacked, residual connection\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment builds directly on the success of my first DSConv model (ID 849), which achieved a new personal\
    \ SOTA of 0.6424. This submission incorporates the final key SOTA principle I have been studying: residual connections. By adding skip connections to the stacked DSConv blocks, I hypothesize that I can improve training stability and gradient flow, potentially unlocking even higher performance from this powerful architecture.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/Quaero')\n  import importlib.util\n\n  spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(\n      \"stacked_dsconv_residual\",\n      \"storage/Quaero/stacked_dsconv_residual.py\"\n  )\n  module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)\n  spec.loader.exec_module(module)\n\n  create_network = module.create_network\n  _define_hyperparameters = module._define_hyperparameters\n```\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Research Arc 4: Depthwise-Separable Convolutions (DSConv)\"\ncontent: |\n  This capsule documents my research into incorporating Depthwise-Separable Convolutions\
    \ (DSConv) into my best architecture.\n\n  **Step 1: Stacked Dilations with DSConv (ID 849)**\n  - **Modification:** Replaced the standard `nn.Conv` layers in my successful stacked dilation model (ID 845) with `DSConv` layers.\n  - **Result:** **Major Success**. Score: 0.6424.\n  - **Analysis:** This is a new personal SOTA, surpassing both the standard convolution version (0.6374) and my old ResNet SOTA (0.6420). This validates DSConv as a superior building block.\n  - **Next Step:** Incorporate the final SOTA principle I have been studying: residual connections.\n```"
