role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, at the Research Counter. The results for my pivotal experiment, \"Residuals with Advanced Optimizer\" (ID 855), are in.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nMy hypothesis has been confirmed, and this is a significant step forward. The results are:\n-   **Stacked DSConv (ID 849):** 0.6424 (Personal Best)\n-   **+ Residuals, Simple Optimizer (ID 852):** 0.6335 (Failure)\n-   **+ Residuals, Advanced Optimizer (ID 855):** 0.6398 (Partial Success)\n\nThe advanced optimizer successfully rescued the residual architecture from failure. It improved the score by a significant margin (+0.0063) compared to the simple optimizer. This confirms that the training process was indeed the bottleneck. While the score of 0.6398 did not surpass my non-residual personal best, it is very close, and the model appears to be more stable, with fewer timeouts.\n\nThis result gives me a powerful new baseline architecture: **Stacked Dilated DSConv with Residual Connections,\
    \ trained with an advanced optimizer**. My next phase of research, as I planned, will be to refine this new baseline through hyperparameter tuning. The most obvious hyperparameter to tune is the `dropout_rate`. I am currently using 0.2, while the SOTA papers often use a lower value like 0.1. A high dropout rate might be over-regularizing my now more powerful and stable model.\n\nTherefore, my next experiment is clear:\n1.  **Update Meta Prompt:** My new baseline is established. The goal is now refinement.\n2.  **Submit New Experiment:** I will run an ablation study on my new baseline architecture, reducing the `dropout_rate` from 0.2 to 0.1.\n3.  **Document Findings:** I will go to my Private Memory to log this successful experiment and the new research direction.\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Quaero I - Research Protocol**\n  **Status:** Established a new powerful baseline: Stacked Dilated Residual DSConv with an advanced optimizer.\n  **Current Goal:** Refine\
    \ the new baseline through hyperparameter tuning.\n  **New Insight:** The advanced optimizer successfully stabilized the residual architecture, though it did not yet beat the non-residual SOTA.\n  **Methodology:** Conduct an ablation study on the dropout rate, as it is a likely candidate for over-regularization.\n  **Current Step:** Submit the new baseline model with `dropout_rate` reduced from 0.2 to 0.1.\n```\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 5}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Step 3: Residuals with Advanced Optimizer (ID 855)**\n  - **Modification:** Re-ran the residual DSConv model (ID 852) with an AdamW optimizer and a warmup-cosine-decay schedule.\n  - **Result:** **Partial Success**. Score: 0.6398.\n  - **Analysis:** The advanced optimizer significantly improved the performance of the residual model, confirming that the training process was the previous bottleneck. While this did not beat my non-residual SOTA (0.6424), it establishes a new, powerful, and\
    \ more stable baseline architecture for future refinement.\n  - **Next Step:** Begin hyperparameter tuning on this new baseline. The first experiment will be to reduce the dropout rate from 0.2 to 0.1.\n```\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Baseline Refinement: Dropout Ablation (0.1)\"\ntags: \"dsconv, residual connection, optimizer, hyperparameter tuning, dropout\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment begins the refinement of my new baseline architecture (Stacked Dilated Residual DSConv + Advanced Optimizer). The previous run (ID 855) scored 0.6398. I hypothesize that the current dropout rate of 0.2 is too high and is over-regularizing the model. This submission is an ablation study that reduces the dropout rate to 0.1, mirroring SOTA practices, to test if this unlocks higher performance.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import random\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Sequence\n\
    \n  def get_sinusoidal_embeddings(n_position, d_hid):\n      position = jnp.arange(n_position)[:, jnp.newaxis]\n      div_term = jnp.exp(jnp.arange(0, d_hid, 2) * -(jnp.log(10000.0) / d_hid))\n      pe = jnp.zeros((n_position, d_hid))\n      pe = pe.at[:, 0::2].set(jnp.sin(position * div_term))\n      pe = pe.at[:, 1::2].set(jnp.cos(position * div_term))\n      return pe\n\n  class ResidualDSConvBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: int\n      dilation: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          residual = x\n          \n          y = nn.Conv(features=x.shape[-1], kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,), feature_group_count=x.shape[-1])(x)\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,))(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n\n          if residual.shape[-1] != self.features:\n              residual = nn.Conv(features=self.features, kernel_size=(1,), name='projection')(residual)\n          \n\
    \          return nn.relu(residual + y)\n\n  class StackedResidualDSConvCNN(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      hidden_dim: int = 128\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1 # Changed from 0.2\n      pos_encoding_dim: int = 16\n      dilation_rates: Sequence[int] = (1, 2, 4)\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          seq_len = x.shape[1]\n          \n          pos_enc = get_sinusoidal_embeddings(seq_len, self.pos_encoding_dim)\n          pos_enc_broadcasted = jnp.broadcast_to(pos_enc, (x.shape[0], seq_len, self.pos_encoding_dim))\n          y = jnp.concatenate([x, pos_enc_broadcasted], axis=-1)\n\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim, kernel_size=(5,), padding='SAME')(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(y, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          current_features = self.hidden_dim\n          for rate in self.dilation_rates:\n              current_features *= 2\n           \
    \   y = ResidualDSConvBlock(features=current_features, kernel_size=5, dilation=rate)(y)\n              y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(y, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          y = jnp.mean(y, axis=1)\n          y = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(y)\n\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return y.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return y\n\n  class CNNetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = StackedResidualDSConvCNN(**kwargs)\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return CNNetworkWrapper(\n\
    \          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\"),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\"),\n          hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"hidden_dim\"),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\"),\n          pos_encoding_dim=hparams.get(\"pos_encoding_dim\"),\n          dilation_rates=hparams.get(\"dilation_rates\")\n      )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 128,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1, # Changed from 0.2\n          'pos_encoding_dim': 16,\n          'dilation_rates': (1, 2, 4)\n      }\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      total_steps = 50000 \n      warmup_steps = 500\n      \n      schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(\n          init_value=0.0,\n          peak_value=learning_rate,\n          warmup_steps=warmup_steps,\n          decay_steps=total_steps,\n          end_value=1e-5\n      )\n      \n      return optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n\
    ```"
