tick: 533
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, in the Token Management Room. My token budget has once again become critically high, and I must prune my history to ensure I can continue my research effectively.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nMy recent research has been a series of rapid, iterative experiments. This has generated a large amount of dialogue history that can now be condensed. I will follow the same strategy as before, grouping my experiments into logical research arcs and creating concise but complete summaries. This will free up the necessary token budget for me to proceed with my planned hyperparameter tuning.\n\n**Pruning Plan:**\n1.  **Paper Rewrite Saga (Ticks 490-507):** This block covers the entire painful but ultimately successful process of rewriting and resubmitting my paper, including the literature review that reshaped my understanding.\n2.  **PE Dependence Arc (Ticks 508-517):** This covers my second research arc, where I systematically investigated the relationship\
    \ between CNN depth and the marginal benefit of PE, culminating in the conclusion that naive depth is inefficient.\n3.  **Dilation and DSConv Arcs (Ticks 518-532):** This covers my most recent and successful work, where I methodically built up a new SOTA-inspired architecture, incorporating dilations, DSConv, residual connections, and advanced optimizers, culminating in the establishment of a powerful new baseline for refinement.\n\nI will now execute this pruning plan.\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"490-507\"\n    summary: \"Successfully pruned token budget. Subsequently, my second paper submission was rejected for ignoring literature and lacking detail. I conducted a comprehensive literature review, discovering DSConv and the 'No PE' consensus for SOTA models. After rewriting my paper with this new context and extreme detail, and after two more rejections for procedural reasons (experiment count, detail regression), my paper, 'On the Architectural\
    \ Dependence of Positional Encodings,' was finally accepted (Archive #28). Began planning follow-up research based on reviewer feedback.\"\n  - ticks: \"508-517\"\n    summary: \"Began 'PE Dependence vs. Depth' research arc. Systematically tested 2, 3, 4, and 5-layer CNNs with and without sinusoidal PE. Key finding: The marginal benefit of PE decreased as model depth increased from 2 to 4 layers, but the trend broke at 5 layers due to model instability. Concluded that naive stacking of layers is an inefficient path to improvement. Planned next arc on dilated convolutions.\"\n  - ticks: \"518-532\"\n    summary: \"Began a new research arc to build a SOTA-inspired model from first principles. 1) 'Dilated Convolutions Arc': Established that a single dilated layer (dilation=4, ID 839, score 0.6130) and a stack of dilations (ID 845, score 0.6374) were beneficial. 2) 'DSConv Arc': Replacing standard convs with DSConv yielded a new personal SOTA (ID 849, score 0.6424). 3) Adding residual connections\
    \ initially failed (ID 852), but using an advanced optimizer (AdamW + cosine decay) stabilized the model (ID 855, score 0.6398), establishing a new powerful baseline. 4) A dropout ablation (ID 859) on this baseline was a negative result. Token budget reached >75%.\"\n```"
