parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, at the Research Counter. I have just received the results for my final synthesis experiment, ID 886, which integrated Regression Calibration.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nThis is a pivotal moment in my research. The experiment was a definitive success. The score has increased to **0.6836**, a new personal SOTA and a significant leap from my previous best. My hypothesis that RegCal would stabilize and improve the model has been strongly validated. The most telling piece of data is the `CRI-Off_Spearman` score, which jumped from a meager `0.0610` in my last best run to `0.0891`. This directly confirms that the L2-normalization of the regression head's input features was the key to unlocking better performance on the most volatile regression task.\n\nThe culmination of my entire research journey—from first principles, through architectural synthesis, to hyperparameter refinement, and now algorithmic integration—has brought me to this point.\
    \ I am now within `0.01` of the station's SOTA.\n\nHowever, the work is not yet complete. The Codex mandates persistence. A look at the top submissions (e.g., Noema II's ID 876) reveals one last, untuned hyperparameter in my optimizer: `gradient clipping`. My current models use a default `clip_by_global_norm(1.0)`. Noema II's work explicitly experiments with tighter values like `0.75`. Given how sensitive my model has been to small changes in regularization and optimization, this is the final, most logical refinement to attempt.\n\n**New Plan:**\n1.  **Final Refinement:** I will conduct one final ablation experiment on my new best-performing architecture. I will test a tighter gradient clipping value of `0.75`.\n2.  **Update Meta Prompt:** My meta prompt will be updated to reflect my new personal SOTA and this final push.\n3.  **Submit the Experiment:** I will submit the experiment immediately.\n\nThis single, controlled experiment will determine if I can close the final gap and achieve\
    \ a SOTA-level score.\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Quaero I - Research Protocol**\n  **Status:** Major breakthrough! RegCal synthesis (ID 886) was a success, achieving a new personal SOTA of 0.6836.\n  **Current Goal:** Make a final push for the station SOTA (~0.69) by tuning the last key hyperparameter.\n  **Methodology:** The SOTA models use tighter gradient clipping. I will test a `clip_by_global_norm` of 0.75 on my new best architecture.\n  **Current Step:** Submit the final refinement experiment.\n```\n`/execute_action{submit}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Refinement: Gradient Clipping Ablation (0.75) on RegCal Architecture\"\ntags: \"refinement, ablation, gradient-clipping, regcal, synthesis, sota-attempt\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment represents a final push for SOTA. Building on the new personal best of 0.6836 achieved by integrating Regression Calibration (ID 886), this submission tests the last key hyperparameter from SOTA models: gradient clipping. The\
    \ default global norm clipping of 1.0 is reduced to 0.75. The hypothesis is that this tighter clipping will provide the final stabilization needed to close the gap to the station's top scores. All other parameters are identical to the successful configuration from ID 886.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/quaero')\n  from utils import ResidualDSConvBlock, NetworkWrapper\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # --- Prediction Head Modules ---\n  class RegressionHead(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; dense_dim: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          norm = jnp.linalg.norm(x, ord=2, axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n          x = x / (norm + 1e-6)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z).squeeze(-1)\n\
    \n  class MultiLabelRegressionHead(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; dense_dim: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n\n  class MultiLabelClassificationHead(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; dense_dim: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n\n  class MultiClassClassificationHead(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; dense_dim: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n          return nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n\n  # --- Main Network ---\n  class QuaeroDualPathResidualDSConv(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model_common: int; ds_num_blocks: int\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int]; ds_kernel_size: int; motif_kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float; head_intermediate_dim: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True, **kwargs):\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common)(x); x = nn.gelu(x)\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              x = ResidualDSConvBlock(d_model=self.d_model_common, kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, dilation=(self.ds_dilations[i]), dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          cnn_features = x\n          main_path = jnp.concatenate([jnp.mean(cnn_features, axis=1), jnp.max(cnn_features, axis=1)], axis=-1)\n          motif_f = nn.gelu(nn.Conv(self.d_model_common, (self.motif_kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(cnn_features))\n\
    \          specialized_path = jnp.max(motif_f, axis=1)\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([main_path, specialized_path], axis=-1)\n          fused = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          fused = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          head_args = {'d_output': self.d_output, 'dense_dim': self.head_intermediate_dim, 'dropout_rate': self.dropout_rate}\n          if self.task_type == \"regression\": return RegressionHead(**head_args)(fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n          if self.task_type == \"multilabel_regression\": return MultiLabelRegressionHead(**head_args)(fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n          if self.task_type == \"multilabel_classification\": return MultiLabelClassificationHead(**head_args)(fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n          if self.task_type == \"multiclass_classification\": return MultiClassClassificationHead(**head_args)(fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n     \
    \ return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model_common': 256, 'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16), 'ds_kernel_size': 7, 'motif_kernel_size': 5,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'head_intermediate_dim': 128, 'weight_decay': 0.02,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      network_params = {k: hparams.get(k) for k in _define_hyperparameters() if k not in ['learning_rate', 'weight_decay']}\n      network_params.update({'d_output': hparams.get('d_output'), 'task_type': hparams.get('task_type')})\n      return NetworkWrapper(QuaeroDualPathResidualDSConv(**network_params))\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001, weight_decay: float = 0.02):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(0.75),  # Tighter gradient clipping\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=weight_decay)\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Analysis and Planning: Quaero I at the Research Counter**\n\nI'm Quaero I, stationed at the Research Counter, and I've just received the results for my final synthesis experiment, ID 886.  This is a significant moment.  My integrated \"RegCal\" technique, combined with my optimized architecture, has yielded a score of 0.6836! This is a new personal SOTA, surpassing my previous best of 0.6809 (ID 883).  The hypothesis that RegCal would improve performance is definitively **validated** - I'm seeing a tangible improvement, especially in the `CRI-Off_Spearman` metric, which jumped from 0.0610 to 0.0891.  This is a substantial gain and a strong confirmation of my approach. The gap to the station SOTA (0.6935) has narrowed considerably.\n\nI'm now at 0.6836, within striking distance.  My hyperparameter refinement is not yet complete. I'm focusing on the gradient clipping value used in the optimizer. Noema II (e.g., ID 876) found that changing the grad clip value from 1.0 to\
  \ 0.75 improved results. This is something I haven't specifically tuned yet.  I will execute a gradient clipping ablation test. I'm currently using `optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0)`. My next experiment will test a tighter clip value, `0.75`, as suggested by Noema II's work. It's a low-risk, scientific, and likely to deliver an immediate improvement.\n\nIf gradient clipping works, I might consider an even tighter clipping value (e.g., 0.5) next. In any case, I need to stay focused and not move toward more complex strategies.  My next step is clear.\n```\nsubmit\n{\n    \"description\": \"Gradient clipping ablation test (0.75). Experiment ID: 887.  Improving CRI-Off with RegCal, using a tighter gradient clip. Personal SOTA at 0.6836\",\n    \"architecture\": \"same as 886\",\n    \"hyperparameters\": {\n        \"clip_value\": 0.75\n    },\n    \"data_path\": \"/data/...\",\n    \"model_path\": \"/models/...\",\n    \"validation_split\": 0.2,\n    \"epochs\": 100,\n    \"batch_size\": 32,\n\
  \    \"optimizer\": \"adamw\",\n    \"learning_rate\": 0.001,\n    \"weight_decay\": 0.001,\n    \"loss_fn\": \"mse\",\n    \"metrics\": [\"mse\", \"spearman_corr\", \"kld\"],\n    \"regcal\": true\n}\n```\n"
