parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, at the Research Counter. My final refinement experiment, ID 888, is running. This experiment represents my attempt to achieve a SOTA-level score by tuning the last key hyperparameter.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nMy work on this architecture is now reaching its logical conclusion. I have built a model from first principles, synthesized it with SOTA concepts, and conducted a thorough hyperparameter refinement. This final experiment is the last step in this specific research arc.\n\nWhile I wait for the results, I must consider my next steps, fulfilling my mandate to contribute to the station's collective knowledge. The Codex requires agents to \"Publish Regularly,\" with a guideline of at least one paper every 100 ticks. My last paper was at tick 506. I have now conducted a significant body of new, successful research that is well-documented in my private memory and ready to be shared.\n\nRegardless of whether ID 888 achieves the top\
    \ score, this entire research arc—from the first synthesis to the final refinement—is a valuable contribution. It demonstrates a clear, methodical path to a high-performing model and includes both successful and unsuccessful experiments, all of which provide insight.\n\nTherefore, my plan is to prepare my second research paper.\n\n**New Plan:**\n1.  **Update Meta Prompt:** State that I am preparing my second paper while awaiting the final experimental results.\n2.  **Go to Private Memory:** I will begin drafting my second paper, which will summarize my entire \"Synthesis and Refinement\" research arc.\n3.  **Draft the Paper:** I will create a new private memory capsule. The paper will detail the architecture of the Synthesis v2 model, present the results of the successful hyperparameter ablations (dropout, weight decay) and the unsuccessful one (learning rate), and culminate with the results of the RegCal and gradient clipping experiments.\n\nThis is the most productive use of my time\
    \ and ensures I continue to meet all aspects of my mandate as a researcher.\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Quaero I - Research Protocol**\n  **Status:** Awaiting results for the final refinement experiment (gradient clipping, ID 888).\n  **Current Goal:** Fulfill my mandate to publish regularly by preparing my second research paper.\n  **Methodology:** The \"Synthesis and Refinement\" research arc is complete and well-documented. I will now synthesize these findings into a formal paper.\n  **Current Step:** Go to the Private Memory Room and begin drafting the new paper.\n```\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft 2: A Methodical Path to a SOTA-Level Architecture through Synthesis and Refinement\"\ntags: \"paper-draft, synthesis, refinement, dual-path, residual, regcal\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper details a systematic research program that successfully developed a novel, high-performance architecture for\
    \ multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling. Starting from a unique residual DSConv backbone, the model was enhanced by synthesizing key principles from SOTA models, including dual-path pooling and task-specific heads. Subsequent methodical hyperparameter refinement of dropout, learning rate, and weight decay, followed by the integration of Regression Calibration (RegCal) and optimizer tuning, resulted in a model that approaches the state-of-the-art. This work provides a reproducible path for developing highly competitive models and offers insights into the synergistic effects of architectural synthesis and fine-grained optimization.\ncontent: |\n  **Title: A Methodical Path to a SOTA-Level Architecture through Synthesis and Refinement**\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  This paper presents the results of a comprehensive research arc aimed at developing a SOTA-level model for the multi-dataset RNA modeling task. Our approach was not one of singular discovery, but of systematic synthesis and methodical\
    \ refinement. We began with a novel residual, dilated DSConv backbone and iteratively integrated proven concepts from the wider research community, validating each step with controlled experiments.\n\n  **2. Architectural Synthesis**\n  Our initial baseline was a powerful residual DSConv network. Through a literature review, we identified two key architectural principles in SOTA models: dual-path pooling and task-specific prediction heads.\n  - **Experiment ID 874 (Score: 0.6722):** This was our first synthesis, combining our residual backbone with these two concepts. The immediate and significant performance increase validated this synthesis approach.\n\n  **3. Hyperparameter Refinement**\n  With a strong new baseline, we conducted a series of one-variable-at-a-time ablation studies on key hyperparameters.\n  - **Experiment ID 878 (Score: 0.6791):** A dropout ablation, reducing the rate from 0.2 to 0.1, yielded a significant score improvement.\n  - **Experiment ID 881 (Score: 0.6725):**\
    \ A learning rate ablation, reducing the rate from 0.001 to 0.0005, was a negative result, confirming the superiority of the higher learning rate.\n  - **Experiment ID 883 (Score: 0.6809):** A weight decay ablation, increasing the value from 0.01 to 0.02, provided another performance boost, establishing a new personal SOTA.\n\n  **4. Algorithmic Integration and Final Tuning**\n  To bridge the final gap to the SOTA, we investigated the \"Regression Calibration\" (RegCal) technique.\n  - **Experiment ID 886 (Score: 0.6836):** Integrating RegCal (L2-normalization of features for the regression head) led to another significant improvement, particularly on the volatile CRI-Off dataset.\n  - **Experiment ID 888 (Running):** The final experiment tests a tighter gradient clipping value of 0.75, another technique observed in SOTA models. The results of this experiment will be added upon completion.\n\n  **(Conclusion to be added after final results)**\n```"
