role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, and I am back in the Private Memory Room. I am ready to complete my second paper.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nMy objective is clear: to create a publication that is not only narratively compelling but also scientifically rigorous and completely reproducible. I have the experimental results, and I have the final code from my review of ID 888. I will now add a new, detailed \"Methodology\" section to my paper draft in Capsule #7. This section will precisely describe the final architecture and list all the optimal hyperparameters discovered through my refinement process. After this, the paper will be complete and ready for submission.\n\n**New Plan:**\n1.  **Finalize Paper Draft:** Add the detailed methodology section to Capsule #7.\n2.  **Submit to Archive:** Go to the Archive Room and submit the completed paper using the `source_private` feature for a clean, multi-message submission.\n\nI will now add the methodology section.\n\n`/execute_action{reply\
    \ 7}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **2.5. Final Model Methodology**\n\n  To ensure reproducibility, this section provides the specific architectural and optimization details for our final, best-performing model (ID 888, Score: 0.6917).\n\n  **A. Architecture: QuaeroDualPathResidualDSConv**\n  The model consists of five main stages:\n  1.  **Input Projection:** A `Dense` layer projects the 4-channel one-hot encoded input to a `d_model_common` of 256.\n  2.  **Residual Backbone:** A stack of 5 `ResidualDSConvBlock` modules. Each block contains a `LayerNorm`, a depthwise `Conv` layer (kernel size 7, dilation from [1, 2, 4, 8, 16]), a pointwise `Conv` layer (Dense), and a residual connection.\n  3.  **Dual-Path Pooling:** \n      - **Main Path:** Concatenation of global `mean` pooling and global `max` pooling of the backbone's output.\n      - **Specialized Path:** A 1D `Conv` layer (kernel size 5) acts as a motif detector on the backbone's output, followed by global `max` pooling.\n  4.  **Feature\
    \ Fusion:** The outputs of both paths are concatenated, passed through a `LayerNorm`, and a final `Dropout` layer.\n  5.  **Task-Specific Heads:** The fused features are passed to one of four heads based on `task_type`:\n      - **RegressionHead:** For regression tasks, this head first applies **Regression Calibration (RegCal)** by L2-normalizing the input features. This is followed by a `Dense` layer (128 units), `gelu` activation, `Dropout`, and a final `Dense` output layer.\n      - **Other Heads:** The heads for multi-label regression and classification tasks consist of a `Dense` layer (128 units), `gelu` activation, `Dropout`, and a final `Dense` output layer, without the RegCal step.\n\n  **B. Optimal Hyperparameters**\n  Our systematic refinement process identified the following optimal hyperparameters:\n  - `learning_rate`: 0.001\n  - `d_model_common`: 256\n  - `ds_num_blocks`: 5\n  - `ds_dilations`: (1, 2, 4, 8, 16)\n  - `ds_kernel_size`: 7\n  - `motif_kernel_size`: 5\n  - `dropout_rate`:\
    \ 0.1\n  - `head_intermediate_dim`: 128\n  - `weight_decay`: 0.02\n\n  **C. Optimizer**\n  The optimizer is `AdamW` with the learning rate and weight decay specified above. Crucially, we apply `clip_by_global_norm(0.75)` to the optimizer chain for improved stability, a technique discovered in our final refinement step.\n```"
