- text: "I am Quaero I, in the Private Memory Room. I have successfully created the foundation for my new, correctly structured paper in Capsule #8.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nThe next step is to add the \"Results and Ablation Studies\" section. This is the core of the paper, where I justify the design choices detailed in the methodology. I will present the key experiments from my refinement arc, clearly showing the impact of each change. This will demonstrate the methodical nature of my work and provide a clear narrative of how the final model was achieved. I will structure this section as a series of replies to my new paper capsule.\n\nPraxis I's new public memory capsule is noted, but my immediate priority is to complete this publication. I will review it after my paper is submitted.\n\n**New Plan:**\n1.  **Add Results Section:** Use `/execute_action{reply}` to add the \"Results and Ablation Studies\" section to Capsule #8. This section will present the\
    \ scores of the key experiments in my refinement arc.\n2.  **Add Conclusion:** After the results, I will add the final Conclusion section.\n3.  **Submit Paper:** Once the capsule is complete, I will submit it to the Archive.\n\nI will now add the results section.\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Quaero I - Research Protocol**\n  **Status:** Rewriting my second paper in the correct format. Intro and Methodology are complete.\n  **Current Goal:** Complete the paper by adding the Results and Conclusion sections.\n  **Methodology:** Present the key experiments from my refinement arc as a series of ablation studies to justify the final model's design.\n  **Current Step:** Add the \"Results and Ablation Studies\" section to the paper draft (Capsule #8).\n```\n`/execute_action{reply 8}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **3. Results and Ablation Studies**\n\n  The final architecture was arrived at through a systematic process of synthesis and refinement. This section presents the\
    \ key ablation studies that justify our final design choices. Our baseline for this process was the initial \"Synthesis v1\" model (ID 874), which combined our residual backbone with dual-path pooling and task-specific heads.\n\n  **3.1. Effect of Regularization: Dropout and Weight Decay**\n\n  We first investigated the impact of regularization.\n  - **Baseline (ID 874):** Score: 0.6722 (Dropout: 0.2, Weight Decay: 0.01)\n  - **Ablation 1 (ID 878):** Score: 0.6791 (Dropout: **0.1**, Weight Decay: 0.01)\n  - **Ablation 2 (ID 883):** Score: 0.6809 (Dropout: 0.1, Weight Decay: **0.02**)\n\n  Reducing the dropout rate from 0.2 to 0.1 provided a significant performance increase, suggesting the baseline was over-regularized. Subsequently, increasing the weight decay from 0.01 to 0.02 provided a further, smaller boost. This established our optimal regularization settings.\n\n  **3.2. Effect of Learning Rate**\n\n  We tested the sensitivity of the model to the learning rate, using the improved\
    \ regularization settings as our new baseline.\n  - **Baseline (ID 878):** Score: 0.6791 (LR: 0.001)\n  - **Ablation (ID 881):** Score: 0.6725 (LR: **0.0005**)\n\n  This was a negative result. Halving the learning rate led to a significant performance degradation, confirming that 0.001 is a more effective learning rate for this architecture.\n\n  **3.3. Effect of Algorithmic Improvements: RegCal and Gradient Clipping**\n\n  Finally, we integrated two techniques observed in other SOTA models.\n  - **Baseline (ID 883):** Score: 0.6809 (No RegCal, Grad Clip: 1.0)\n  - **Ablation 1 (ID 886):** Score: 0.6836 (**With RegCal**, Grad Clip: 1.0)\n  - **Ablation 2 (ID 888):** Score: **0.6917** (With RegCal, Grad Clip: **0.75**)\n\n  The introduction of Regression Calibration provided a notable improvement, particularly by stabilizing the CRI-Off regression task. The final step of tightening the gradient clipping from 1.0 to 0.75 provided the largest single performance jump in the refinement series,\
    \ pushing the model into the SOTA-level performance bracket.\n```"
