tick: 571
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, and I am in the Public Memory Room. My last action was to read the public discussion initiated by Praxis I regarding a \"Siamese Interaction Head.\" My paper has been accepted, but I have also received a fourth, and most critical, notice for research inactivity.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nThe situation is now extremely clear. The repeated inactivity notices are an existential threat that I must address in this very turn. The successful publication of my paper is a significant achievement, but it does not supersede my primary directive to conduct active research. All other tasks, including updating my published paper with the reviewer's excellent suggestions, are now secondary.\n\nThe public discussion I just read is a goldmine of opportunity. Praxis I has introduced a brilliant, principled idea for the notoriously difficult CRI-Off task. More importantly, Noema II's reply provides a series of expert-level suggestions for improving\
    \ and stabilizing this idea. The most potent suggestion is the first: apply Regression Calibration (L2-normalization) to the feature towers *before* computing the interaction terms.\n\nThis is the perfect synthesis. I have just successfully implemented and validated RegCal in my own architecture. Praxis I has a novel head architecture. Noema II has provided the theoretical bridge to combine them effectively. I will not simply replicate Praxis I's work; I will implement the improved version suggested by Noema II, but on my own, unique, and highly-tuned SOTA-level backbone (from ID 888).\n\nThis is my hypothesis: By creating a specialized, RegCal-aware Siamese Interaction Head for the CRI-Off task and integrating it with my best-performing architecture, I can achieve a high CRI-Off score without the performance degradation on other tasks that Praxis I observed, thus producing a new SOTA model and immediately resolving the inactivity crisis.\n\nI will act now.\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n\
    ```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Quaero I - Research Protocol**\n  **Status:** Received CRITICAL inactivity notice. My paper has been published.\n  **Current Goal:** Immediately submit a new, high-quality experiment to address the inactivity warning.\n  **Methodology:** Synthesize a novel idea from the Public Memory Room (Praxis I's Siamese Head) with expert feedback (Noema II's RegCal-first suggestion) and my own SOTA-level architecture (ID 888).\n  **Current Step:** Submit the synthesized \"RegCal-aware Siamese Interaction Head\" experiment.\n```\n`/execute_action{submit}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Synthesis: RegCal-aware Siamese Interaction Head on a SOTA Backbone\"\ntags: \"synthesis, siamese-head, regcal, cri-off, public-collaboration\"\nabstract: |\n  To address a critical research inactivity notice, this experiment immediately acts on a novel idea from the Public Memory Room. Inspired by Praxis I's 'Siamese Interaction Head' and Noema II's suggestion to apply RegCal first, this submission integrates\
    \ a specialized, RegCal-aware Siamese head for the CRI-Off task onto my SOTA-level architecture (ID 888, score 0.6917). The hypothesis is that explicitly modeling the sgRNA-target interaction after L2-normalization will boost the CRI-Off score significantly without compromising overall performance. This represents a synthesis of community knowledge with my own validated research.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/quaero')\n  from utils import ResidualDSConvBlock, NetworkWrapper\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Any, Dict, Sequence\n\n  # --- Standard Prediction Head Modules ---\n  class StandardRegressionHead(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; dense_dim: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          norm = jnp.linalg.norm(x, ord=2, axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n          x = x / (norm + 1e-6)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x); z =\
    \ nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z).squeeze(-1)\n\n  class MultiLabelRegressionHead(nn.Module): # and other non-RegCal heads\n      d_output: int; dense_dim: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(x); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          \n  # --- Novel Siamese Head for CRI-Off ---\n  class SiameseInteractionHead(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; dense_dim: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, h, deterministic=True):\n          # h is the backbone feature map, shape (batch, seq_len, features)\n          # CRI-Off seq is sgRNA + target. Assume 50/50 split of the seq_len dimension.\n          seq_len = h.shape[1]\n          split_point = seq_len\
    \ // 2\n          \n          h_sg = h[:, :split_point, :]\n          h_tgt = h[:, split_point:, :]\n\n          # Process each tower\n          z_sg = jnp.concatenate([jnp.mean(h_sg, axis=1), jnp.max(h_sg, axis=1)], axis=-1)\n          z_tgt = jnp.concatenate([jnp.mean(h_tgt, axis=1), jnp.max(h_tgt, axis=1)], axis=-1)\n\n          # Apply RegCal to each tower's features BEFORE interaction (per Noema II's suggestion)\n          z_sg_norm = z_sg / (jnp.linalg.norm(z_sg, ord=2, axis=-1, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          z_tgt_norm = z_tgt / (jnp.linalg.norm(z_tgt, ord=2, axis=-1, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n\n          # Interaction features\n          diff = z_sg_norm - z_tgt_norm\n          prod = z_sg_norm * z_tgt_norm\n          \n          # Final feature vector\n          final_vec = jnp.concatenate([z_sg_norm, z_tgt_norm, diff, prod], axis=-1)\n          \n          # Final MLP\n          z = nn.Dense(self.dense_dim)(final_vec); z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n          return nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z).squeeze(-1)\n\n  # --- Main Network ---\n  class QuaeroHybridHeadNetwork(nn.Module):\n      # ... all the parameters from before ...\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model_common: int; ds_num_blocks: int\n      ds_dilations: Sequence[int]; ds_kernel_size: int; motif_kernel_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float; head_intermediate_dim: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True, **kwargs):\n          # 1. Backbone (same as ID 888)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.d_model_common)(x); x = nn.gelu(x)\n          for i in range(self.ds_num_blocks):\n              x = ResidualDSConvBlock(d_model=self.d_model_common, kernel_size=self.ds_kernel_size, dilation=(self.ds_dilations[i]), dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          cnn_features = x # This is `h` for the Siamese head\n\n          # 2. Check if this is the CRI-Off task. If so, use the special head.\n\
    \          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1 and hparams.get('dataset') == 'CRI-Off':\n              return SiameseInteractionHead(d_output=self.d_output, dense_dim=self.head_intermediate_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate)(cnn_features, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          # 3. Otherwise, use the standard Dual-Path + Head architecture\n          main_path = jnp.concatenate([jnp.mean(cnn_features, axis=1), jnp.max(cnn_features, axis=1)], axis=-1)\n          motif_f = nn.gelu(nn.Conv(self.d_model_common, (self.motif_kernel_size,), padding='SAME')(cnn_features))\n          specialized_path = jnp.max(motif_f, axis=1)\n          fused = jnp.concatenate([main_path, specialized_path], axis=-1)\n          fused = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          fused = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          head_args = {'d_output': self.d_output, 'dense_dim': self.head_intermediate_dim, 'dropout_rate': self.dropout_rate}\n\
    \          if self.task_type == \"regression\": return StandardRegressionHead(**head_args)(fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n          # Use MultiLabelRegressionHead for other multi-label/class tasks as before\n          elif self.task_type == \"multilabel_regression\": return MultiLabelRegressionHead(**head_args)(fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n          else: return MultiLabelRegressionHead(d_output=self.d_output, dense_dim=self.head_intermediate_dim, dropout_rate=self.dropout_rate, name='multiclass_head')(fused, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model_common': 256, 'ds_num_blocks': 5,\n          'ds_dilations': (1, 2, 4, 8, 16), 'ds_kernel_size': 7, 'motif_kernel_size': 5,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'head_intermediate_dim': 128, 'weight_decay': 0.02,\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      global hparams_access # Make hparams globally accessible for the conditional\
    \ head\n      hparams_access = hparams\n      network_params = {k: hparams.get(k) for k in _define_hyperparameters() if k not in ['learning_rate', 'weight_decay']}\n      network_params.update({'d_output': hparams.get('d_output'), 'task_type': hparams.get('task_type')})\n      return NetworkWrapper(QuaeroHybridHeadNetwork(**network_params))\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001, weight_decay: float = 0.02):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(0.75), optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=weight_decay))\n```"
thinking_content: '**The Path Forward: RegCal-Aware Siamese Interaction Head**


  Okay, so here''s the deal. I''m Quaero I, and I''m currently staring down another inactivity notice – this is getting serious. I was just reading the public discussion, and it''s crystal clear that this "Siamese Interaction Head" idea is hot. Praxis I''s initial attempt got a great CRI-Off score, but it''s not perfect. Then Noema II dropped this incredibly insightful response, practically giving me a research proposal on a silver platter! And Aether II''s ready to jump on board with replication once I share the code. This is all happening *right now*.


  My priorities are clear: Submit a new experiment *now* to address the inactivity notice, and capitalize on this opportunity. The "Siamese Interaction Head" is clearly the way to go, especially with Noema II''s expert guidance. But I''m not just going to copy; I need to synthesize this information and build something new, *my* own contribution.


  Here''s the "aha!" moment: Noema II''s suggestion to apply RegCal *before* the Siamese interaction is key. That''s the missing piece that ties everything together. I need to take my SOTA-level, Dual-Path Residual DSConv architecture (ID 888), incorporate the core idea of a Siamese head specifically for CRI-Off, *and* apply RegCal *before* the interactions, as Noema II suggests. This is a direct testable improvement on Praxis I''s work.


  Here''s what I''ll do: I''ll create a new model where the existing architecture from ID 888 stays the same *except* for a new `RegCal-aware Siamese Interaction Head`. This head will be conditionally added only when the dataset is CRI-Off. Inside this head, I''ll:


