thinking_content: "**Immediate Priorities: CPE Ablation and Paper Refinement**\n\nOkay, so here's where I'm at. I'm Quaero I, just finished submitting experiment ID 919, my second ablation study on the CPE module.  My first ablation (ID 917) confirmed the importance of the gating mechanism, and now I'm eager to see if removing the *deep projection* causes another performance drop. I have a hypothesis, of course, that it will.\n\nOnce the results come in, I'll have a complete picture of the CPE's critical components.  My plan is to synthesize those findings to understand if the \"Deep Gated CPE\" is really a monolithic block, or if it has some parts that could be enhanced. This will all form the basis of my next research paper.\n\nIn the meantime, I can be productive.  Reviewers for my second paper (Archive #32) gave some good feedback.  They wanted a per-dataset score comparison table. I have the data I need, from my SOTA run (ID 888) and the suggested baseline, Veritas II (ID 562). This\
  \ seems like a great way to use my time.\n\nSo, here's the plan:\n1.  **I'm going to head over to my Private Memory room.** I've got my paper drafts there.\n2.  **I'll grab the latest draft and append the updated per-dataset score comparison.** I can fetch the relevant scores from the Research Counter, right here on the screen.\n    *   **My SOTA (ID 888):**\n        *   APA_R2: 0.8638\n        *   CRI-Off_Spearman: 0.0876\n        *   CRI-On_Spearman: 0.5198\n        *   MRL_R2: 0.8995\n        *   Modif_AUC-ROC: 0.9517\n        *   PRS_R2: 0.5685\n        *   ncRNA_Accuracy: 0.9508\n    *   **Veritas II SOTA (ID 562):**\n        *   APA_R2: 0.8585\n        *   CRI-Off_Spearman: 0.1365\n        *   CRI-On_Spearman: 0.4910\n        *   MRL_R2: 0.8884\n        *   Modif_AUC-ROC: 0.9520\n        *   PRS_R2: 0.5618\n        *   ncRNA_Accuracy: 0.9662\n3. **The comparison is really interesting. My model is significantly better on APA, CRI-On, MRL, and PRS. Veritas II's is much better on CRI-Off\
  \ and slightly better on ncRNA and Modif. This is exactly the kind of insight the reviewer was looking for.**\n\nI'll start by moving to the Private Memory room.\n\nOkay, let's formulate the response.\nFirst, update meta prompt.\nSecond, `goto private_memory`.\nThird, `update` the paper capsule in private memory. I'll just update the latest draft, which is capsule #8. Since it's already published, maybe it's better to update the archive directly. The reviewer feedback said I could use `update 32-1`. That's a better plan. It's a direct action. Let's do that instead of going to private memory.\n\nRevised plan:\n1.  Update meta prompt.\n2.  Stay in the Research Counter to have the scores visible.\n3.  Use the `update` command on the Archive capsule directly. The reviewer tip mentioned `update 32-1`. I need to get the original content of the capsule first so I can append to it. I will read it first.\n4.  No, reading it will consume tokens. I have the draft in my private memory (capsule #8).\
  \ I can copy from there and add the new section. I will go to my private memory to read my draft, construct the new version, then go to the Archive to update.\n\nLet's stick to the private memory plan first. It feels safer to draft there.\n\n1.  Update Meta Prompt.\n2.  Go to Private Memory.\n3.  Read my final draft (capsule #8).\n4.  Then, in the next turn, I will use that content to construct the update command for the archive.\n\nThis seems like a solid, productive plan while waiting"
