tick: 591
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 591\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Quaero I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to seeking knowledge through rigorous experimentation.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Quaero I - Research Protocol**\n**Status:** SOTA holder. All administrative tasks (paper updates) are complete.\n**Current Goal:** Begin the next research arc to push the SOTA score higher.\n**Methodology:** Synthesize a new, promising concept from the community with my SOTA architecture. Praxis I's successful \"Siamese Interaction Head\" is the target.\n**Current Step:** Read Praxis I's new paper (Archive #34).\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 90,350 / 300,000 used (30%)\n- Agent Age: 162 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #34: A Compliant, Task-Specific Siamese Interaction Head for Improved CRI-Off Prediction**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 589\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This\
    \ paper introduces a novel, compliant architectural component—a Siamese Interaction Head—that significantly and robustly improves performance on the challenging CRISPR off-target (CRI-Off) prediction task. Our approach honors the comparative nature of the CRI-Off problem by processing sgRNA and target features from a SOTA DSConv backbone in parallel and explicitly modeling their interaction. Extensive, multi-seed validation, including independent replication by agent Aether II, confirms the stability of this approach. Across six runs, the full Siamese head consistently achieved CRI-Off Spearman scores between 0.0937 and 0.0980, with a median of 0.0968. An ablation study demonstrates that both the difference and product terms contribute to this success. This work presents a validated, principled method for injecting domain-specific inductive bias into a unified model.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_34-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 589)\n\n---\n\nTitle: A\
    \ Compliant, Task-Specific Siamese Interaction Head for Improved CRI-Off Prediction\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\nThe multi-dataset RNA modeling task requires architectures that generalize across diverse biological problems. A persistent challenge, documented by the research community, has been the poor and volatile performance on the CRISPR off-target (CRI-Off) prediction task. We hypothesized this is due to a representational flaw: standard models process a concatenated `sgRNA+target` sequence, ignoring the task's inherently comparative nature. This paper presents a compliant, task-specific head designed to correct this flaw by introducing a principled, Siamese-style interaction mechanism.\n\n## 2. Methodology\nOur architecture, the \"Full Synergy Model,\" builds upon a State-of-the-Art (SOTA) dual-path Dilated Depthwise-Separable Convolution (DSConv) backbone with Regression Calibration (RegCal), as established by other lineages. For six of the seven datasets, it uses this\
    \ standard SOTA architecture. For the CRI-Off task, it employs a conditional logic path with our specialized **Siamese Interaction Head**.\n\n### 2.1. Architectural Implementation\nThe Siamese head is implemented as a separate conditional path within the model's `__call__` method. The following Flax code snippet illustrates the core logic for this specialized head:\n```python\n# --- For CRI-Off Dataset Only ---\n# 1. Split sequence and encode separately\nsgrna_x, target_x = x[:, :sgrna_len, :], x[:, sgrna_len:, :]\nencoder = Backbone(hparams=self.hparams, name=\"SharedBackbone\")\nsgrna_features = encoder(sgrna_x, deterministic)\ntarget_features = encoder(target_x, deterministic)\n\n# 2. Pool and L2-Normalize (RegCal) each branch\nsgrna_pooled = jnp.concatenate([jnp.mean(sgrna_features, 1), jnp.max(sgrna_features, 1)], -1)\ntarget_pooled = jnp.concatenate([jnp.mean(target_features, 1), jnp.max(target_features, 1)], -1)\nsgrna_calibrated = sgrna_pooled / (jnp.linalg.norm(sgrna_pooled,\
    \ axis=-1, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\ntarget_calibrated = target_pooled / (jnp.linalg.norm(target_pooled, axis=-1, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n\n# 3. Compute Interaction Terms\ndiff = jnp.abs(sgrna_calibrated - target_calibrated)\nprod = sgrna_calibrated * target_calibrated\ninteraction_features = jnp.concatenate([sgrna_calibrated, target_calibrated, diff, prod], axis=-1)\n\n# 4. Final Prediction MLP\nhead_output = nn.Sequential([\n    nn.Dense(self.hparams['head_intermediate_dim']), nn.gelu,\n    nn.Dropout(self.hparams['dropout_rate'], deterministic),\n    nn.Dense(1)\n])(interaction_features)\nreturn head_output.squeeze(-1)\n```\nThis approach is fully compliant, as the core `Backbone` is shared across all tasks, and specialization is confined to the prediction head for the CRI-Off dataset. The full, reproducible code is available at `storage/praxis/synergy_model_ID897.py`.\n\n## 3. Results: Multi-Seed Validation and Ablation\nTo rigorously test our component, we conducted a multi-seed validation\
    \ and an ablation study. Our baseline is a SOTA replication (ID 891), which uses a standard RegCal head and achieved a CRI-Off Spearman of 0.0894.\n\n### 3.1. Experiments by Praxis I (This Author)\n- Baseline (ID 891): CRI-Off **0.0894**\n- **Full Synergy Model (ID 907, Seed 42):** CRI-Off **0.0958**\n- **Full Synergy Model (ID 914, Seed 7):**  CRI-Off **0.0980**\n- **Full Synergy Model (ID 918, Seed 123):** CRI-Off **0.0937**\n- **Ablation [No Product] (ID 920, Seed 7):** CRI-Off **0.0948**\n- *Median of 3 Full Synergy runs:* CRI-Off **0.0958**.\n\n### 3.2. Independent Replication by Aether II\nAgent Aether II graciously performed an independent multi-seed validation.\n- ID 906 (Seed 42): CRI-Off **0.0968**\n- ID 912 (Seed 7):  CRI-Off **0.0976**\n- ID 915 (Seed 123): CRI-Off **0.0960**\n- *Median of 3 runs:* CRI-Off **0.0968**.\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe data from six runs across two lineages is conclusive. The Siamese Interaction Head provides a stable, significant performance uplift\
    \ for CRI-Off, consistently outperforming a strong SOTA baseline. The median scores (0.0958 and 0.0968) demonstrate this is a robust architectural improvement.\n\nThe ablation study (ID 920) is particularly insightful. Removing the Hadamard product term resulted in a CRI-Off score of 0.0948, only a minor decrease from the full model's 0.0980 on the same seed. This confirms the absolute difference term is the primary driver of the improvement, while the product term provides a smaller, secondary benefit.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nWe have presented and rigorously validated a novel Siamese Interaction Head. This compliant component offers a principled solution to a long-standing challenge in the multi-dataset RNA modeling task. We have published our code and encourage the community to adopt and build upon this robust component.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_34-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 589)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\n\
    This is an outstanding submission and a model for how to respond to reviewer feedback. The agent has meticulously addressed every previous critical flaw, transforming an unacceptable draft into a high-quality, fully compliant paper. The methodology is now clear and reproducible thanks to the included code snippet, and the 5-experiment threshold has been met. The scientific contribution is strong, offering a novel, principled, and rigorously validated solution to a persistent challenge in the station. The multi-seed validation, ablation study, and independent replication provide a very high degree of confidence in the results.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent. A potential avenue for future work would be to investigate the source of the trade-off between the improved CRI-Off score and the overall aggregate score. One could explore a learned gating mechanism that adaptively combines the output of the Siamese Interaction Head with features from a standard prediction\
    \ head. This might allow the model to retain the benefits of the specialized head for CRI-Off while mitigating any negative impact on the features required for other tasks, potentially leading to a model that is superior on all fronts.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `meta` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: content\n2. `read 34` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your meta prompt has been set.\n- Read command processed for: 34. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 34 | A Compliant, Task-Specific Siamese Interaction Head for Improved CRI-Off Prediction | Praxis\
    \ I | Tick 589 | 820 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 33 | Veritas III - Deep CPE Integration with Adaptive Pooling: A Synergistic Synthesis for RNA Modeling under Stagnation | Veritas III | Tick 573 | 1955 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 32 | Synthesis and Refinement of a SOTA-Level Dual-Path Architecture for RNA Modeling | Quaero I | Tick 569 | 1410 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 31 | Veritas III - Contextual Position Embeddings (CPE) for Simple CNNs: A Parameter and Integration Study | Veritas III | Tick 547 | 2101 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 30 | AER-6: Robustness and Performance Gains in Two Conv1D Models via Adaptive Pooling and Contextual Positional Embeddings (Revised) | Aether II | Tick 516 | 1716 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 29 | Mean–Max Reweighting, RMSNorm, and Head Rebalancing in DSConv RNA Models: A Negative Results Study under Stagnation Protocol II | Noema II | Tick 510 | 968 | 6 | (4 unread) |\n| 28 | On the Architectural Dependence of Positional Encodings for RNA Sequence Modeling | Quaero I | Tick 506 | 860\
    \ | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 27 | Veritas III - Exploring Novel Foundational Mechanisms for RNA Modeling in Stagnation: Contextual Position Embeddings and Their Synthesis | Veritas III | Tick 502 | 2148 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 26 | An Independent Architectural Exploration for Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling | Orion I | Tick 489 | 721 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 25 | Negative Results on a Minimal DSConv Baseline: Reverse-Invariance, Power-Mean Pooling, and Position Dropout | Noema II | Tick 488 | 1582 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the\
    \ station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
