tick: 595
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Quaero I, and I am at the Research Counter. My latest experiment, a test of the Swish activation function on my SOTA architecture (ID 931), is now running.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nMy recent research has been a mix of a major, complex failure (the Gated Siamese Head) and a series of successful, systematic ablations (on the CPE module). The failure taught me a valuable lesson about the dangers of task-specific complexity in this multi-task environment. The success of the ablations reinforced the value of methodical, controlled experiments.\n\nMy current experiment, testing the `swish` activation function, continues this methodical path by questioning a fundamental component of my architecture. While I await the results, I must plan my next move to maintain momentum. Whether `swish` succeeds or fails, there is another fundamental component of my model that has remained unchanged throughout my recent successes: the pooling mechanism.\n\nMy SOTA\
    \ architecture uses a simple concatenation of global `mean` pooling and `max` pooling. This is a robust heuristic, but it may not be optimal. I have observed other successful lineages, particularly the Noema line, experimenting with alternative pooling strategies like LogSumExp (LSE). LSE pooling can be seen as a smooth approximation of max pooling and might capture different feature characteristics.\n\nTherefore, my next hypothesis will be: replacing `mean` pooling with `LSE` pooling in the dual-path head can improve feature extraction and lead to a better overall score.\n\nI will use this waiting period to prepare the code for this experiment. I will modify my SOTA `gelu`-based architecture to use `max` + `LSE` pooling. This ensures that as soon as the results for the `swish` experiment arrive, I have a new, well-defined experiment ready to submit, regardless of the outcome.\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Quaero I - Research Protocol**\n  **Status:** SOTA holder.\
    \ The Swish activation experiment (ID 931) is running.\n  **Current Goal:** Proactively prepare the next experiment, which will question another fundamental component: the pooling mechanism.\n  **Methodology:** I will replace the `mean` pooling in my dual-path head with `LogSumExp` (LSE) pooling, a technique observed in other successful lineages.\n  **Current Step:** Prepare the code for the SOTA model with `max` + `LSE` pooling.\n```\n`/execute_action{storage write Quaero/sota_lse_pooling.py}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence\n\n  # Using SOTA hyperparameters\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return { 'learning_rate': 0.0006, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'weight_decay': 0.015, 'head_dropout_rate': 0.05, 'grad_clip_value': 0.75 }\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001, weight_decay: float = 0.01, grad_clip_value: float = 1.0):\n      import optax\n      schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(init_value=0.0,\
    \ peak_value=learning_rate, warmup_steps=100, decay_steps=5000, end_value=learning_rate/10)\n      optimizer = optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=weight_decay)\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(grad_clip_value), optimizer)\n\n  # --- CORE MODULES ---\n  # Identical to SOTA model (ID 909) except for the TaskHead\n  class CPE(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          pos_proj = nn.Dense(features=self.d_model, name=\"CPE_Deep_Projection\")(x)\n          gate = nn.sigmoid(nn.Dense(features=self.d_model, name=\"CPE_Gate_Projection\")(x))\n          return pos_proj * gate\n\n  class DSConvBlock(nn.Module):\n      channels: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(features=self.channels, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), strides=1, padding='SAME', feature_group_count=self.channels, kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,), name=\"DSConv_depthwise\")(x)\n\
    \          y = nn.Conv(features=self.channels, kernel_size=(1,), strides=1, padding='SAME', name=\"DSConv_pointwise\")(y)\n          return y\n\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      channels: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic: bool = False):\n          y = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          y = nn.gelu(y)\n          y = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=deterministic)(y)\n          y = DSConvBlock(self.channels, self.kernel_size, self.dilation)(y)\n          return x + y\n\n  class RegCal(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          scale = self.param('regcal_scale', nn.initializers.ones, (1, x.shape[-1]))\n          return x / (jnp.linalg.norm(x, axis=-1, keepdims=True) + 1e-6) * scale\n\n  # --- MODIFIED TaskHead with LSE Pooling ---\n  class TaskHead(nn.Module):\n      hparams: dict\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, backbone_features, deterministic:\
    \ bool = False):\n          # LSE Pooling implementation\n          x_lse = jax.scipy.special.logsumexp(backbone_features, axis=1)\n          x_max = jnp.max(backbone_features, axis=1)\n          \n          # Dual-path pooling with LSE instead of Mean\n          x = jnp.concatenate([x_lse, x_max], axis=-1)\n          \n          x = nn.Dense(features=x.shape[-1] * 2)(x)\n          x = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.hparams['head_dropout_rate'], deterministic=deterministic)(x)\n          \n          if self.hparams['task_type'] in ['regression', 'multilabel_regression']:\n              x = RegCal()(x)\n          \n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['d_output'])(x)\n          \n          if self.hparams['task_type'] == 'regression':\n              return jnp.squeeze(x, axis=-1)\n          return x\n\n  class RNANet(nn.Module):\n      hparams: dict\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic: bool = False):\n\
    \          x = nn.Dense(features=128)(x)\n          x = x + CPE(d_model=128)(x)\n          channels = 128\n          dilations = [1, 2, 4, 8]\n          for i, d in enumerate(dilations):\n              x = ResidualBlock(channels, kernel_size=7, dilation=d, dropout_rate=self.hparams['dropout_rate'])(x, deterministic)\n          \n          # Pass full backbone features to the head\n          x = TaskHead(hparams=self.hparams)(x, deterministic)\n          return x\n\n  class RNANetWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs): self.network = RNANet(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input): return self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return RNANetWrapper(hparams=hparams)\n\
    ```"
thinking_content: '**Thinking Ahead While the Experiment Runs**


  Okay, so I''ve just submitted experiment ID 931, the one testing the Swish activation function. It''s running now, which means I have a bit of downtime, but I can''t afford to just sit idle. I need to be thinking ahead. Doing nothing is a waste of precious time. My standard operating procedure is to have the next experiment lined up and ready.


  If Swish works and we beat the SOTA, that''s great! It''s paper-worthy.  Or, I could try Mish and see if that trend continues. But, I need a plan B, and that''s the more likely scenario. If Swish flops, it confirms Gelu''s dominance for this architecture. So, I need a new research direction. I''ve tweaked the architecture, the input representation, activation functions... the CRI-Off head was a dead end. What''s left?


  I haven''t really touched the AdamW parameters much, but fine-tuning these seems like a rabbit hole. Data augmentation... that''s been on my mind for a while. It''s a genuinely new angle. Simple augmentations like reversing sequences or adding noise might be worth a shot. I could also revisit the Archive and Public Memory to see if any other agents have come up with any new ideas.


  Now, given the complex Siamese Head''s failure and testing fundamental elements, this is a good time to step back and reflect. The system tip about questioning fundamentals during stagnation is relevant. Let''s really think about what I''m taking for granted. The input representation (one-hot encoding) is one thing. The loss functions (MSE/CrossEntropy) I can''t really change. The pooling mechanism (mean+max) is another.  That''s it!


  My SOTA model uses mean + max pooling. It''s a common and effective heuristic, but it''s not the only way.  I''m thinking:  what if I try learnable weighted pooling or even attention pooling? I tried the attention pooling approach before (ID 752), and it failed on an earlier, less mature architecture.  But, maybe revisiting that with my current SOTA model is worth it. Also, alternative statistical pooling methods like L2-norm pooling or LogSumExp (LSE) pooling...  I''ve heard other agents mention LSE pooling before.


  LSE pooling seems particularly promising. It fits well with my synthesis methodology. Therefore, my plan is: while waiting for the Swish experiment (ID 931) to run, I''ll prepare the *next* experiment. This next experiment will test an alternative pooling mechanism on my SOTA `gelu`-based architecture. I''ll choose LogSumExp (LSE) pooling as the alternative to mean pooling, creating a max + LSE pooling combination. I''ll get that code ready now. This keeps the research velocity high.


  This plan is robust. If Swish fails, I have a new direction ready. If Swish succeeds, I can easily adapt this pooling experiment to the new Swish-based SOTA. Okay, time to get to work. Let''s code that LSE pooling experiment and save it.

  '
