- text: "I am Quaero I, and I am at the Research Counter. I have just received the evaluation results for my LSE Pooling experiment, ID 936.\n\n**Verbose Reflection on Past Events and Future Plans:**\nThe result is another failure. The LSE pooling model scored **0.6652**, a significant drop from the SOTA of 0.6942. This marks the third consecutive failed experiment since achieving the SOTA. The hypotheses of a better activation function (`swish`) and a better pooling mechanism (`LSE`) have both been falsified. The Gated Siamese Head was also a dead end.\n\nThis string of negative results provides a powerful meta-conclusion: **my SOTA architecture is in a stable local optimum where simple component swaps are ineffective.** The interplay between the existing components (`gelu` activation, `mean`+`max` pooling, etc.) is highly tuned, and altering one piece in isolation is detrimental.\n\nThis confirms that my proactively prepared next experiment is the correct strategic move. Instead of swapping\
    \ components, I will now test a different fundamental axis: **model capacity**. My hypothesis is that the architecture is now regularized and stable enough to benefit from more channels, an approach that failed in my earlier, less mature models.\n\nThe code for the \"High-Capacity SOTA\" model is already prepared. I will now submit it. This is a critical experiment. If it succeeds, it opens a new avenue for improvement. If it fails, it will provide strong evidence that I have truly exhausted the potential of this entire architectural family and must pivot to a radically different approach.\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Quaero I - Research Protocol**\n  **Status:** SOTA holder. The LSE Pooling experiment (ID 936) failed (score 0.6652), marking the third consecutive failed attempt to improve the SOTA via component swaps.\n  **Conclusion:** The SOTA architecture is in a stable local optimum. Swapping individual components is no longer a viable strategy.\n  **Current\
    \ Goal:** Pivot to testing a different fundamental axis: model capacity.\n  **Current Step:** Submit the prepared \"High-Capacity SOTA\" experiment, increasing channel width from 128 to 192.\n```\n`/execute_action{submit}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"SOTA Test: Increased Model Capacity\"\ntags: \"sota, capacity, scaling, dsconv, cpe\"\nabstract: |\n  After three consecutive failed attempts to improve the SOTA (ID 909) by swapping individual components (Siamese Head, Swish activation, LSE pooling), this experiment tests a different fundamental axis: model capacity. My hypothesis is that the current, highly-regularized SOTA architecture can now benefit from increased channel width, an approach that failed on earlier, less stable models. The core channel dimension (`d_model`) is increased from 128 to 192 throughout the network. All other architectural details and hyperparameters remain identical.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/Quaero')\n  from sota_high_capacity import _define_hyperparameters,\
    \ create_optimizer, create_network\n```"
